window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol3/03_test_time_compute"] = {"key": "vol3/03_test_time_compute", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "Limits of a Single Response"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 3, "title": "Internal budgets and external orchestration"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "How reasoning models spend output allowances"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 2, "title": "Selecting from a Candidate Pool"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Useful variation among candidates"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 3, "title": "Choosing a selection rule"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "Candidate availability under pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Balancing pool size against checking cost"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 2, "title": "Verifying Candidates"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 3, "title": "Checking whole outputs vs. intermediate steps"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 3, "title": "How base rates limit check precision"}, {"id": "sec-13", "level": 3, "title": "How search exploits imperfect checks"}, {"id": "sec-14", "level": 3, "title": "Preventing revision thrashing"}, {"id": "sec-15", "level": 2, "title": "Bounded Search"}, {"id": "sec-16", "level": 3, "title": "Where each search structure prunes"}, {"id": "sec-17", "level": 3, "title": "How step pruning bounds tree growth"}, {"id": "sec-18", "level": 3, "title": "Reserving budget before admitting work"}, {"id": "sec-19", "level": 3, "title": "When to stop searching early"}, {"id": "sec-20", "level": 2, "title": "Evaluating Deliberation Policies"}, {"id": "sec-21", "level": 3, "title": "Controlled and system-level comparisons"}, {"id": "sec-22", "level": 3, "title": "Policies under a deadline"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Test-Time Compute</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>When is another token, candidate, check, or model call worth its cost?</em></p>\n<p>An agent that deliberates before acting pays latency and money for a better chance of success, whether it spends them on a longer derivation, more candidate patches, an extra check, or another model call. Each purchase answers a different question. More tokens re-examine what the prompt already holds, more candidates raise the chance that a good one sits somewhere in the pool, and only an executed check adds a fact the model did not have. None of them makes the returned answer correct, because a selector can return a plausible failure, and a search that optimizes against a flawed check finds the flaw faster than the fix. Deliberation is therefore a budgeted allocation problem with a stopping rule. The runtime decides where the next unit of work goes, how far a passing check can be trusted, and when to stop, abstain, or escalate instead of returning its best unverified guess. Every extra turn spent inside one decision lengthens the horizon, so that spending has to be bounded before the agent acts on anything.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Compare depth, breadth, and feedback as ways to spend a per-decision budget, by the information each adds, the tokens it consumes, and its critical path.</li>\n<li>Distinguish candidate availability from selected-output quality when evaluating voting, learned scoring, and task-specific checks.</li>\n<li>Calculate accepted-set precision from stated checker error rates and explain how adaptive search can change those assumptions.</li>\n<li>Represent a candidate plan as a dependency graph and identify which parts require revision after an observation changes.</li>\n<li>Analyze how step-level checks and pruning bound a search tree's growth and its scorer cost.</li>\n<li>Design stopping rules that enforce resource ceilings without treating budget exhaustion as acceptance evidence.</li>\n<li>Evaluate competing deliberation policies under a common budget and deadline by pass probability, actual spend, and abstentions.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">Limits of a Single Response</h2>\n<p>The repair agent (@sec-vol3-intro-lighthouses) inspects the connection-pool implementation and receives a completed candidate patch. The invocation contract admits it as a candidate, yet the patch rests on an incomplete diagnosis. Client requests intermittently stall even after idle connections become available. Two distinct failure modes can produce this symptom: an aggressive timeout that aborts too early, or a lost notification when a worker thread returns a connection to the pool. When prompted, the language model proposes extending the timeout. That change offers a plausible workaround, but it provides no evidence that the timeout caused the stalls. Deploying such a patch immediately risks masking the latency tail while leaving the concurrency defect intact.</p>\n<p>Before committing to an edit, the agent runtime can spend extra work in three distinct ways. It can give the model a longer output allowance to trace how threads acquire and release the lock step by step. It can sample several candidate patches that modify different parts of the pooling logic. It can also run a targeted test harness to record whether a waiting thread actually receives the condition-variable notification. Each option buys a different kind of evidence. Extended reasoning can expose logical flaws in the source text. Sampling across temperature or prompt variations can surface fixes that a greedy single pass misses. An instrumented test, by contrast, observes runtime state directly and reveals dynamic thread interactions that token generation alone cannot establish.</p>\n<p>This division marks the boundary between model computation and runtime observation. Extra model computation re-evaluates information already present in the prompt. Within a single generation, a model can backtrack and revise an earlier analytical misstep even as the previous tokens remain frozen in its prefix. Pure computation, however, cannot create new empirical facts. Only an executed test reports how the running process behaves, and its conclusions hold only for the conditions and thread schedules that the test harness exercised.</p>\n<p>Suppose the targeted test forces an interleaving where one worker thread waits while another returns a connection. The trace shows that the connection enters the pool, yet the waiting worker stays blocked past the notification point. That observation rules out the timeout hypothesis immediately and points the agent toward the signaling path. The agent can then write a patch that repairs the notification logic and test it against the failing reproducer. Passing a targeted reproducer still does not establish full task acceptance, because narrow tests can miss edge cases, and neither an unchecked hypothesis nor an isolated micro-test is enough to ship code to production. Even so, the two paths part ways before any file changes (@fig-vol3-diagnostic-tree). Acting on the first guess hides the symptom and keeps the defect, whereas checking first spends one short test to learn which hypothesis survives.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ PROBE AND GENERATION ALLOWANCES (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: nbk-03-probe-versus-generation.\n# │ Goal: Compare a short diagnostic operation with an output allowance.\n# │ Show: Generation time and output charge, distinct from probe duration.\n# │ How: Reuse the registry weight-read allowance from Foundation Model;\n# │      exclude prefill, queueing, transport, and probe setup overhead.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\n\nclass ProbeAllocation:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    scenario = Scenarios.ProbeVsGeneratedExplanation  # 8-bit Llama-3 70B decode floor, one H100\n    answer_tokens = scenario.answer_tokens  # hypothetical output length\n    probe_time = scenario.probe_time  # hypothetical already-prepared test duration\n    output_price = scenario.output_price  # hypothetical dollars per million output tokens\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    result = scenario.result\n    token_time = result.token_time\n    answer_time = result.answer_time\n    answer_cost = result.answer_cost\n    time_ratio = result.time_ratio\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(answer_time &gt; probe_time &gt; 0 * second, &quot;Positive scenario durations&quot;)\n    check(answer_cost &gt; 0 * USD, &quot;Output has a positive scenario charge&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    answer_tokens_str = fmt_int(answer_tokens)\n    probe_time_str = fmt_time(probe_time, unit=&quot;ms&quot;, precision=0)\n    token_time_str = fmt_time(token_time, unit=&quot;ms&quot;, precision=2)\n    answer_time_str = fmt_time(answer_time, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=2)\n    answer_cost_str = fmt_usd(answer_cost, precision=5)\n    output_price_str = fmt_usd((output_price * Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0)\n    time_mult_str = fmt_multiple(time_ratio, precision=1)\n]```\n\n::: {#nbk-03-probe-versus-generation .callout-notebook title=&quot;A probe vs. a longer answer&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: A prepared diagnostic test takes `[ProbeAllocation.probe_time_str]`. A proposed explanation contains `[ProbeAllocation.answer_tokens_str]` output tokens. How long does the explanation take to generate relative to the test?\n\n**Variables**: The illustrative per-token generation time from @sec-vol3-foundation-model-per-call-work-estimate is `[ProbeAllocation.token_time_str]`, derived from memory-bandwidth limits rather than a measured production service rate. The hypothetical output pricing is `[ProbeAllocation.output_price_str]` per million tokens. Prompt processing, queueing, transport, test preparation, and the downstream model call that reads the test observation are excluded.\n\n**Math**: Generating `[ProbeAllocation.answer_tokens_str]` tokens at `[ProbeAllocation.token_time_str]` per token requires `[ProbeAllocation.answer_time_str]` and incurs `[ProbeAllocation.answer_cost_str]` in token charges. Dividing this generation latency by the `[ProbeAllocation.probe_time_str]` test run yields a factor of `[ProbeAllocation.time_mult_str]`.\n\n**Systems insight**: When the unresolved question concerns dynamic program behavior, the probe settles it `[ProbeAllocation.time_mult_str]` faster than the model can generate a speculative explanation of it, and only the probe&#x27;s answer is an observation. Running a test is preferable whenever its preparation and execution time remain below the explanation&#x27;s `[ProbeAllocation.answer_time_str]` and its outcome cleanly separates the competing failure modes. Conversely, an inconclusive test that does not separate the hypotheses provides no diagnostic gain regardless of its speed. Failure to reproduce can still supply negative evidence when the test&#x27;s sensitivity to the suspected fault is known. A sound runtime spends work on whichever operation most cheaply removes the main source of uncertainty.\n\n:::\n\nThe probe, and the decision to revise the patch on its evidence, both happened outside any single model call. The invocation contract of the previous chapter ends at one prompt and one candidate, accompanied only by metadata indicating whether generation reached a stop token or an output limit. That interface cannot decide whether the next second of latency or fraction of a cent is better spent on longer reasoning, extra candidate samples, or a runtime probe. An API response marked complete confirms only that generation finished, not that the proposed patch fixes the bug. **Test-time compute** is all the system work the runtime orchestrates around the model call, with model weights held fixed, to generate, test, and select candidates before committing to an action. Governing that spend requires a runtime policy that sets hard ceilings on latency and cost, routes work across reasoning and checks, and stops cleanly without mistaking an exhausted budget for a verified solution.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The bounded single-invocation model contract is established in @sec-vol3-foundation-model-contract.\n:::\n\nEach part of that policy raises the next question. The runtime first chooses how to spend extra work across reasoning depth, candidate breadth, and empirical feedback (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-computation-allocation). Breadth produces a pool, so the runtime must then pick from it without being fooled by a plausible failure (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-candidate-diversity), and picking well depends on how far a passing check can be trusted (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-verification). When a check refutes a premise partway through a plan, the runtime should regenerate only what depended on it (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-planning-revision). Candidates and revisions together form a search whose spending must be tracked, reserved, and stopped (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-bounded-search), and the complete policy is finally judged against its alternatives under one budget and one deadline (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-strategy-design).\n\n## Three Axes of Test-Time Compute {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-computation-allocation}\n\nGiving a repair agent a four-thousand-token allowance does not determine its latency, memory footprint, or diagnostic reach, because the runtime can spend those same four thousand tokens on one long derivation, four parallel candidate patches, or three edit–test–revise rounds. Although all three options can consume the same number of output tokens, they stress the serving system in different ways. A long derivation generates tokens one after another along a single causal chain. Independent candidate patches can run concurrently across accelerator hardware. An edit–test–revise loop must pause model generation while each diagnostic test runs. Those dependencies determine which operations sit on the critical latency path and how much state the serving engine must hold in memory.\n\n**Depth** extends sequential computation within a single candidate, **breadth** explores multiple candidate paths concurrently, and **feedback** folds environment observations into subsequent revisions. A **test-time deliberation rollout**} is one bounded candidate-generation path, potentially interleaving verification checks and observation-driven revisions; the term comes from game search, where a rollout plays one line of moves forward to its end. Depth lengthens a rollout, breadth runs several in parallel, and feedback inserts checks between a rollout&#x27;s turns. An allocation policy can combine all three: it might generate several candidate patches in parallel, run regression tests against each, and spend deeper step-by-step reasoning only on the most promising survivor. Throughout, model weights remain frozen, and every axis draws from the same test-time budget.\n\n::: {#dfn-vol3-test-time-compute-budget .callout-definition title=&quot;Test-time compute budget&quot;}\n\n***Test-time compute budget*** is the per-decision allowance of processed prompt tokens, generated tokens, model invocations, verification checks, and elapsed wall-clock time that a runtime may expend, with model weights fixed, before it must select a candidate, abstain, or escalate to a human operator.\n\n1.  **Significance**: The budget sets the attempt cost in the effective cost per accepted task, $\\CostPerAcceptedTask = \\mathbb{E}[\\AttemptCost]/\\AcceptanceProbability$ (@eq-vol3-cost-per-accepted-task). Additional deliberation reduces cost per accepted task when it increases the acceptance rate $\\AcceptanceProbability$ proportionally faster than it increases the expected attempt cost. A higher cost per accepted task can still be justified when it buys the required acceptance rate within the deadline (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-operating-point).\n2.  **Distinction**: Training compute amortizes weight optimization once across all future tasks. Test-time compute recurs on every decision, where an internal reasoning allowance is merely one control surface alongside external tool invocations and verification checks.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Treating the maximum allocation ceiling as the expected spend, or treating an exhausted budget as evidence that the best remaining candidate is acceptable.\n\n:::\n\nWhat separates the three axes is their dependency structure (@fig-vol3-allocation-axes). Depth extends a single candidate through multi-step reasoning, breadth fans out to sampled candidates that converge on a selector, and feedback routes a candidate through a sandboxed check whose observation conditions the revision.\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-allocation-axes fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Three compute allocation topologies**: Depth extends one candidate; breadth exposes separate sampled candidate paths followed by selection; feedback makes a later candidate depend on an observed check result. The arrows show dependencies, not guaranteed latency, cost, or correctness.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Three panels labeled Depth, Breadth, and Feedback. Depth connects an input through a single chain of four reasoning segments to a candidate. Breadth fans an input out to three separate two-segment sample paths, bracketed as sampled paths, that converge on a selector, which outputs a candidate. Feedback sends a candidate into a dashed sandbox region containing a check, which produces an observation; a revised candidate receives one arrow from the observation and one from the original candidate.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n### Extending reasoning along the depth axis {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-depth-axis}\n\nAsking a model to jump straight from a bug report to a patch often misses multi-step lock interactions, yet letting it reason for thousands of tokens puts every step onto the critical latency path and can exhaust the output limit before any code is emitted. Extending a call along the depth axis lets an autoregressive model unroll intermediate reasoning tokens before emitting its final decision or artifact, generalizing early scratchpad techniques and chain-of-thought prompting [@nye2021show; @wei2022cot]. In the connection-pool scenario, these intermediate tokens might trace thread interleavings step by step: acquiring the pool mutex, checking for leaked socket descriptors, and signaling waiter condition variables. Because autoregressive generation conditions on all prior tokens, later decoding steps can inspect earlier derivations, catch flawed assumptions, and change course before emitting code. That gain comes entirely from running extra forward passes over the growing context rather than changing the network weights.\n\nOn the hardware, however, extending a single generation path serializes the work. Because transformer decoding at small batch sizes is memory-bandwidth bound, step latency is set by the time needed to stream all model parameters from high-bandwidth memory into processor caches (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-memory-bandwidth-decoding}). Wall-clock generation time therefore scales roughly linearly with the number of generated tokens under a fixed serving setup. A long reasoning trace shows only that the accelerator performed more work, not that the resulting code compiles or runs correctly. The runtime must also leave enough output budget for the final patch: a derivation that hits the token limit mid-thought returns a truncated response, which the invocation contract quarantines like any other incomplete call rather than admitting as a candidate.\n\n### Parallel sampling along the breadth axis {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-breadth-axis}\n\nDrawing a single completion commits the repair to whatever hypothesis the model&#x27;s first tokens favored, and if that initial framing is wrong, extra depth only elaborates a flawed premise. Sampling along the breadth axis hedges against that commitment by requesting multiple candidate patches from the same source excerpt and task specification using stochastic decoding controls. Conditional on a fixed prompt and independent random seeds, these samples are statistically independent draws from the model&#x27;s output distribution. Even so, they remain bounded by what the model knows: if the model assigns negligible probability mass to a valid thread-notification pattern, every independent sample can repeat the same missing broadcast. Independent draws can still fail in identical ways.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Temperature, top-$\\Glyph{k}$, and nucleus sampling controls are established in @sec-vol3-foundation-model-sampling-controls.\n:::\n\nBecause parallel candidates do not depend on one another, breadth exposes fine-grained concurrency at modest latency cost. Under memory-bandwidth-bound decoding (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-memory-bandwidth-decoding}), a batch of $\\CandidatePoolSize$ concurrent candidate streams reads the model weights once per step for all $\\CandidatePoolSize$ tokens. Spending an allowance of $\\CandidatePoolSize \\times \\OutputLength$ tokens sequentially along the depth axis multiplies generation latency by roughly $\\CandidatePoolSize$. Generating $\\CandidatePoolSize$ candidates of length $\\OutputLength$ in parallel, by contrast, keeps total generation time close to the duration of the longest candidate, $\\max_n \\DecodeInterval(\\OutputLength_n)$, provided the batch stays memory-bandwidth bound and fits in accelerator memory.\n\nAccelerator memory is where breadth extracts its price. Each active generation stream must maintain its own key-value (KV) cache for the tokens it generates. When the serving system shares prefixes across streams, the $\\SequenceLength$-token prompt is stored once in memory, and peak KV cache demand scales as\n\n$$\n\\BreadthPeakKvCacheMemory \\propto \\SequenceLength + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\CandidatePoolSize} \\OutputLength_n\n$$\n\nWithout shared prefix caching, the serving engine stores $\\CandidatePoolSize$ copies of the prompt&#x27;s KV tensors, and memory demand scales as $\\CandidatePoolSize \\SequenceLength + \\sum_n \\OutputLength_n$. When total KV cache demand exceeds hardware capacity, the serving engine must split candidates into sequential waves. Once forced into waves, breadth latency degrades from the duration of the single longest candidate toward the sum across waves, while memory thrashing and queueing delays stretch individual token latencies. Whether identical prompt prefixes share physical memory or receive provider billing discounts depends on the serving engine.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Block sharing, which stores a common prompt&#x27;s KV state once for every candidate that branches from it, is developed in @sec-vol3-kv-cache-cow-prefix-sharing.\n:::\n\nBreadth also shifts the burden downstream, because generating a wide pool of candidates pays off only if the runtime can tell which candidate is best. A pool that holds a working patch is useless if the selector cannot identify it. In a study of repeated sampling, @brown2024monkeys showed that the probability that at least one candidate is correct (pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$) kept rising across four orders of magnitude of samples. In contrast, practical selectors such as majority voting and learned reward models (scorers trained to predict whether a candidate is correct, examined in @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-outcome-step-evidence) plateaued after a few hundred samples. Candidate selection is therefore a major consumer of compute and verification budget, not an incidental final step.\n\n### Revising from observations along the feedback axis {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-feedback-axis}\n\nNeither a longer chain of thought nor a wider pool of samples tells the model whether a waiting thread actually receives a condition-variable signal at runtime. The feedback axis supplies that missing evidence by executing a check on a candidate and feeding the resulting observation into the next model request. Instead of relying on frozen weights and a static prompt alone, the repair agent proposes a candidate patch, the runtime runs a bounded test suite, and the next model call receives concrete diagnostics such as a stack trace from a deadlocked worker thread. Those runtime observations narrow the next candidate far more sharply than asking the model to reread the original prompt on its own [@shinn2023reflexion].\n\nVerification checks return structured outcomes: failure, pass, or inconclusive execution (such as a test timeout). A failure trace points to broken assertions or regressions; a passing test confirms that the code satisfies the exercised assertions under the tested conditions; and final acceptance remains a separate policy gate. Running diagnostics is itself an external action that consumes CPU and memory, runs inside a sandbox, and adds wall-clock latency even when used only inside deliberation.\n\nEach feedback round is also one more turn of the horizon $\\TurnHorizon$, which ties this axis to horizon compounding (@eq-vol3-horizon-compounding). Unchecked turns compound error as $(1-\\PerStepErrorRate)^{\\TurnHorizon}$, and the introduction showed (@sec-vol3-intro-horizon-arithmetic) that a check which rejects a bad step and retries it resets that compounding. Feedback is the one axis that places such a check inside every turn, so it can lengthen a deliberation without the unchecked compounding its extra turns would otherwise carry, to the extent that its checks deserve trust (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-verification).\n\nFeedback forces strict serialization because the next revision cannot start until the current check finishes and returns its observation. Context policies that append every prior candidate and diagnostic report also make the input prompt grow from turn to turn. For an initial prompt length $\\BasePromptLength$, candidate lengths $\\OutputLength_j$, and observation lengths $\\lvert \\TrajectoryObservation_j\\rvert$, an append-only policy yields an input length at turn $\\TurnIndex$ of\n\n$$\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)} = \\BasePromptLength + \\sum_{j=1}^{\\TurnIndex-1}(\\OutputLength_j + \\lvert \\TrajectoryObservation_j\\rvert)$$\n\nThis is the append-only growth that @eq-vol3-trajectory-resend priced across a whole trajectory, now confined to one decision, and it drives up prompt prefill latency, accelerator memory usage, and token billing on every round. Context-compaction methods can summarize earlier attempts or drop stale diagnostic traces, but the allocation policy must budget for the full token volume actually sent to the model API.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Context-compaction methods and their scheduling within the request budget are developed in @sec-vol3-context-engineering-compaction.\n:::\n\n### The three axes under one output ceiling {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-common-output-ceiling}\n\nCapping generated tokens at a single number does not cap wall-clock latency, accelerator memory, or input-token billing, because depth, breadth, and feedback turn the same output ceiling into different resource loads. Runtimes therefore track four quantities across the three axes: input and output token counts, critical-path wall-clock latency, peak retained accelerator state, and external information gained. Let $\\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength)$ be the time required to ingest an $\\SequenceLength$-token prompt, $\\PerTokenDecodeTime$ the per-token decoding step time, and $\\DecodeInterval(\\OutputLength)$ the total generation time for $\\OutputLength$ tokens. Let $\\FeedbackCheckDuration$ be the duration of the external check in feedback round $\\TurnIndex$, which is also the turn index of the trajectory. With $\\ExtendedDepthLength$ tokens of extended depth, $\\CandidatePoolSize$ breadth candidates executed concurrently, and $\\TurnHorizon$ feedback turns, the latency dimension decomposes into three critical paths:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\DepthCriticalPathLatency &amp;\\approx \\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength) + \\ExtendedDepthLength\\,\\PerTokenDecodeTime,\\\\\n\\BreadthCriticalPathLatency &amp;\\approx \\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength) + \\max_{n} \\DecodeInterval(\\OutputLength_n),\\\\\n\\FeedbackCriticalPathLatency &amp;\\approx \\sum_{t=1}^{\\TurnHorizon}\\bigl(\\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)}) + \\DecodeInterval(\\OutputLength_t) + {\\FeedbackCheckDuration}_{\\TurnIndex}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$ {#eq-vol3-allocation-critical-path}\n\nThe critical path for breadth assumes the serving cluster has enough memory and compute capacity to decode all $\\CandidatePoolSize$ streams concurrently at an unchanged step time. When capacity saturates, decoding falls back into serialized waves and shifts the critical path toward the sum across candidate streams. The feedback expression, by contrast, grows from two sources: sequential check durations $\\FeedbackCheckDuration$ and lengthening prompt prefill times $\\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)})$ as context accumulates. @Eq-vol3-allocation-critical-path covers only latency; @tbl-vol3-allocation-model sets those critical paths beside the tokens processed, the peak model state retained, and the external information acquired.\n\n| **Axis** | **Tokens processed**                                                                                        | **Critical path**                  | **Peak retained model state**                                                                  | **New information**                      |\n|:---------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------|\n| Depth    | $\\SequenceLength + \\ExtendedDepthLength$                                                                    | One dependent sequence             | One context of $\\SequenceLength + \\ExtendedDepthLength$ tokens                                 | None beyond the request and weights      |\n| Breadth  | $\\SequenceLength + \\sum_n \\OutputLength_n$ (prompt shared)                                                  | Longest candidate, given capacity  | $\\CandidatePoolSize$ contexts, $\\SequenceLength + \\sum_n \\OutputLength_n$ tokens if shared     | Sampling variation over the same request |\n| Feedback | $\\sum_{t}(\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)} + \\OutputLength_t)$, with $\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)}$ growing | Sum of turns, including each check | One active context of $\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnHorizon)} + \\OutputLength_{\\TurnHorizon}$ tokens | Observations from executed checks        |\n\n: **Resource model of the three allocation axes**: Symbolic accounting of processed tokens, critical path, retained model state, and information source. Concurrency and prefix sharing are capacity and implementation assumptions, not properties of the axis. {#tbl-vol3-allocation-model tbl-colwidths=&quot;[11,23,22,25,19]&quot;}\n\nOf the three axes, only feedback brings in external information absent from the model weights and prompt. The worked comparison evaluates these expressions for the connection-pool repair under a fixed output ceiling of 4,096 tokens, showing how equal output ceilings produce different latencies and token bills.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ COMMON OUTPUT CEILING, DIFFERENT EXECUTION TOPOLOGIES (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: nbk-03-matched-budget-axes.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Separate output ceilings, actual spend, and dependent elapsed time.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Input/output counts, hypothetical bills, and generation/check time.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Hold model allowance fixed; stipulate four concurrent breadth streams.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│      Feedback appends each prior candidate and report; no cache discounts.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import *</p>\n<p>class AllocationAxes:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>scenario = Scenarios.OutputCeilingThreeAllocations  # 8-bit Llama-3 70B decode floor, one H100 prompt = scenario.prompt  # hypothetical fully serialized initial input tokens ceiling = scenario.ceiling  # common total generated-token ceiling candidates = scenario.candidates  # breadth concurrency and candidate count candidate_tokens = scenario.candidate_tokens  # hypothetical generated length per short candidate rounds = scenario.rounds  # hypothetical feedback rounds actually used report = scenario.report_tokens  # tokens appended per complete observation check_time = scenario.check_time  # hypothetical duration per feedback check price_in = scenario.input_price  # hypothetical per-million-input-token price price_out = scenario.output_price  # hypothetical per-million-output-token price</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>result = scenario.result depth_input, depth_output = result.depth_input, result.depth_output breadth_input, breadth_output = result.breadth_input, result.breadth_output feedback_input, feedback_output = result.feedback_input, result.feedback_output depth_time, breadth_time, feedback_time = result.depth_time, result.breadth_time, result.feedback_time depth_cost, breadth_cost, feedback_cost = result.depth_cost, result.breadth_cost, result.feedback_cost path_ratio = result.path_ratio bill_ratio = result.bill_ratio breadth_premium = result.breadth_premium  # (N - 1) P re-read prompt tokens</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(depth_output == breadth_output == ceiling, \"Depth and breadth exhaust ceiling\") check(feedback_output &lt; ceiling, \"Feedback uses less than the output ceiling\") check(feedback_input == 16_380, \"Append-only feedback input accounting\") check(depth_time &gt; feedback_time &gt; breadth_time, \"Scenario critical-path ordering\") check(breadth_premium == (candidates - 1) * prompt, \"Breadth premium is the re-read prompt\") check(bill_ratio &gt; 1 and path_ratio &gt; 1, \"Breadth buys path length with bill\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>prompt_str = fmt_int(prompt) ceiling_str = fmt_int(ceiling) candidates_str = fmt_int(candidates) candidate_tokens_str = fmt_int(candidate_tokens) rounds_str = fmt_int(rounds) report_str = fmt_int(report) check_time_str = fmt_time(check_time, unit=\"s\", precision=1) price_in_str = fmt_usd((price_in <em> Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0) price_out_str = fmt_usd((price_out </em> Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0) depth_input_str = fmt_int(depth_input) depth_output_str = fmt_int(depth_output) breadth_input_str = fmt_int(breadth_input) breadth_output_str = fmt_int(breadth_output) feedback_input_str = fmt_int(feedback_input) feedback_output_str = fmt_int(feedback_output) depth_time_str = fmt_time(depth_time, unit=\"s\", precision=2) breadth_time_str = fmt_time(breadth_time, unit=\"s\", precision=2) feedback_time_str = fmt_time(feedback_time, unit=\"s\", precision=2) depth_cost_str = fmt_usd(depth_cost, precision=5) breadth_cost_str = fmt_usd(breadth_cost, precision=5) feedback_cost_str = fmt_usd(feedback_cost, precision=5) path_mult_str = fmt_multiple(path_ratio, precision=0) bill_mult_str = fmt_multiple(bill_ratio, precision=1) breadth_premium_str = fmt_int(breadth_premium) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A repair request starts with <code>[AllocationAxes.prompt_str]</code> input tokens and a common ceiling of <code>[AllocationAxes.ceiling_str]</code> generated tokens. How do depth, breadth, and feedback spend that allowance?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>: Reuse the illustrative per-token generation time from the preceding example. Breadth has capacity for <code>[AllocationAxes.candidates_str]</code> concurrent streams at that same rate. Each short candidate uses <code>[AllocationAxes.candidate_tokens_str]</code> generated tokens. Feedback uses <code>[AllocationAxes.rounds_str]</code> turns, each followed by a <code>[AllocationAxes.check_time_str]</code> check returning <code>[AllocationAxes.report_str]</code> tokens. Later feedback requests append earlier candidates and reports. Hypothetical prices are <code>[AllocationAxes.price_in_str]</code> per million input tokens and <code>[AllocationAxes.price_out_str]</code> per million output tokens, without cache discounts, so each breadth stream is billed for its own copy of the prompt (unlike the shared-prefix row of @tbl-vol3-allocation-model). Generation times omit prefill, queueing, and transport; the feedback time additionally includes its checks. The bill counts model tokens only, and @tbl-vol3-allocation-tradeoffs tallies each allocation's tokens, time, and bill side by side.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Allocation</th><th>Input tokens</th><th>Output tokens</th><th>Generation and included checks</th><th>Model-token bill</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Depth</td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.depth_input_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.depth_output_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.depth_time_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.depth_cost_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Breadth</td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.breadth_input_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.breadth_output_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.breadth_time_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.breadth_cost_str]</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Feedback</td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.feedback_input_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.feedback_output_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.feedback_time_str]</code></td><td><code>[AllocationAxes.feedback_cost_str]</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Allocation accounting under a common output ceiling</strong>: Equal ceilings produce unequal spend. Allowances omit prefill, queueing, selection, and tool charges. {#tbl-vol3-allocation-tradeoffs}</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: In @tbl-vol3-allocation-tradeoffs, depth generates all <code>[AllocationAxes.ceiling_str]</code> tokens along a single path. Breadth generates <code>[AllocationAxes.candidate_tokens_str]</code> tokens on each of <code>[AllocationAxes.candidates_str]</code> concurrent paths, shortening the critical path by <code>[AllocationAxes.path_mult_str]</code>. However, reading the prompt <code>[AllocationAxes.candidates_str]</code> times across unshared streams adds <code>[AllocationAxes.breadth_premium_str]</code> input tokens, multiplying its billing cost to <code>[AllocationAxes.bill_mult_str]</code> that of depth. Feedback accumulates input context over $\\TurnHorizon$ turns according to $\\TurnHorizon\\,\\BasePromptLength + (\\OutputLength + \\lvert \\TrajectoryObservation\\rvert)\\,\\TurnHorizon(\\TurnHorizon-1)/2$, reaching <code>[AllocationAxes.feedback_input_str]</code> tokens at $\\TurnHorizon =$ <code>[AllocationAxes.rounds_str]</code> because each successive round re-reads all prior candidates and observation reports.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Breadth trades money for lower wall-clock latency, and its entire billing premium comes from the $(\\CandidatePoolSize-1)\\SequenceLength$ re-read prompt tokens. The governing ratio is prompt length relative to generated output: a large repository context makes breadth's input bill grow steeply with both $\\CandidatePoolSize$ and $\\SequenceLength$, whereas a short prompt or a shared prefix cache removes most of that surcharge. Feedback lands between depth and breadth in both latency and billing while remaining the only axis that acquires new runtime observations. Because this accounting leaves out pass rates, it prices the work rather than the quality of the result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h3\">Internal budgets and external orchestration</h3>\n<p>When a model service hides its intermediate reasoning tokens behind an API, a runtime that gauges effort from the length of the visible reply understates both token spend and latency. Production systems therefore separate two control surfaces: internal model deliberation and external workflow orchestration. A model service may expose an internal budget that caps how many intermediate tokens a single call may spend, while the runtime independently controls how many calls to dispatch, which tool checks to run, and what context to pass into subsequent requests. Internal budgets adjust depth inside one call; external orchestration governs breadth across branches, schedules sandboxed checks, and assembles revised prompts. Because the service may redact intermediate tokens from the returned text, the runtime must read explicit token-usage headers and termination status codes rather than character counts to enforce quotas and track spend.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">How reasoning models spend output allowances</h3>\n<p>That internal budget governs <strong>reasoning models</strong>, which can otherwise burn an entire output allowance on intermediate steps and return an empty or truncated final answer. Trained with reinforcement learning on tasks with objective, verifiable outcomes, such as formal mathematics, logic puzzles, and unit-tested codebases, these models learn to generate extended intermediate scratchpads that propose solutions, check steps, and backtrack within a single call [@openai2024o1; @deepseekai2025deepseekr1]. At the serving layer, those intermediate reasoning tokens are ordinary autoregressive output tokens that consume accelerator compute, fill the KV cache, and incur full output-token charges. Because that scratchpad draws from the call's output allowance, services expose a reasoning-budget parameter that turns sequential depth from a fixed model habit into a per-request runtime knob. The orchestrator can set that knob by task, yet internal reasoning still operates strictly over the supplied prompt and static weights. An extended scratchpad can check arithmetic or trace logic across the prompt, but it cannot observe runtime behavior or tell whether a proposed patch fixes a connection leak without running a test suite.</p>\n<p>↪ Reinforcement learning on tasks with verifiable outcomes, the training that produces reasoning models, is developed in @sec-vol3-rlvr.</p>\n<p>Because models trained with verifiable rewards spend output allowances differently than instruction-tuned models (models adapted to follow example instructions and responses), token ceilings must be calibrated per model family, and call telemetry guides orchestration policy without guaranteeing that a completed candidate is correct. Treating depth as a per-call knob alongside external breadth and feedback still lets the runtime vary spend across tasks, raising the practical question of which allocation axis pays off at each level of task difficulty.</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario</strong>: @snell2024scaling evaluated strategies for spending a fixed test-time compute budget across reasoning benchmarks, comparing sequential revision of a single candidate against parallel breadth sampling and search guided by learned process verifiers (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-verification). The study grouped benchmark problems into difficulty tiers by the base model's single-shot success rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong>: On easier problems, sequential revision used the budget most effectively, fixing minor mistakes without the overhead of parallel sampling. On moderately difficult problems, hybrid strategies combining parallel exploration with verifier-guided search reached higher accuracy. On the hardest problems, scaling test-time compute hit diminishing returns across all axes. At matched total FLOPs, a well-chosen test-time allocation let a smaller model beat a model 14 times larger whenever the smaller model had a nonzero base success rate. When base success sat near zero, spending FLOPs on a larger base model beat exhausting tokens on parallel rollouts.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: With the supplied prompt held fixed, extra reasoning and sampling can surface capabilities the model already has, but they cannot supply missing runtime facts. Feedback can bring new observations into the next call, though their value still depends on the check's coverage and the model's ability to use them. Runtimes should route budgets by estimated difficulty: spend a small budget or a single call on straightforward tasks, use parallel search and feedback on moderately hard tasks, and escalate near-zero-yield requests to a stronger model or a human operator instead of burning tokens on unguided sampling.</p>\n<p>Because a runtime does not know a task's true difficulty in advance, it estimates difficulty with cheap, fast signals before committing a large deliberation budget. Common signals include agreement among two or three short pilot samples, fast static linters, and historical failure rates on similar requests. When pilot samples agree and pass static checks, the runtime keeps the budget small and finishes in one call. When pilot samples disagree or linters flag errors, the scheduler escalates to broader sampling or an edit–test–revise loop, and if the budget ceiling is reached without a verified candidate, it stops and escalates to a human operator. Each routing signal acts as a classifier whose precision and recall should be measured on held-out tasks.</p>\n<p>Internal depth and external breadth work together within a single search policy. Each rollout may use enough depth to draft a coherent code patch while the runtime samples several such patches in parallel and filters them through a test harness. Even so, the mix has to fit the system's bottlenecks: slow tests stretch the feedback critical path, and weak tests dull the value of each observation. Weak checks carry an even sharper cost when reused during training, because reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards uses them as a training reward. Any blind spot or false pass that causes a bad selection at test time therefore becomes a loophole that training actively optimizes for on every weight update.</p>\n<p>↪ How an acceptance check becomes a training reward, and how its gaps are exploited during training, is developed in @sec-vol3-rlvr-rewards.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Information</strong>: For the connection-pool repair, which axis can separate a short timeout from a missed notification, and why can the other two not?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Concurrency</strong>: If the service admits only one candidate at a time, how long does the notebook's breadth allocation take to generate, and which allocation then has the shortest path?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Breadth premium</strong>: How many extra input tokens does <code>[AllocationAxes.candidates_str]</code>-way breadth read compared with depth for a <code>[AllocationAxes.prompt_str]</code>-token prompt, and what happens to that premium when the repository context grows eightfold?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Evidence</strong>: If a revised patch passes a targeted test, what additional information determines whether the task may stop?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choosing how to spend compute across depth, breadth, and feedback determines what candidates enter the pool, but generating a good candidate does not help if the selector picks a plausible broken one instead. The next step is choosing among candidates without spending more on selection than the pool is worth.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h2\">Selecting from a Candidate Pool</h2>\n<p>Sampling eight candidate patches for the connection pool helps nothing if seven of them repeat the same broken timeout workaround and the selector picks one of the seven over the single patch that fixes the notification bug. In a typical pool, one candidate might lengthen an acquisition timeout, another might add exponential retries, and a third might change how the connection-return path wakes waiting threads. The runtime has to decide which proposal, if any, has enough supporting evidence to return, and gathering that evidence consumes time and tokens from the same deliberation budget that produced the pool.</p>\n<p>Two questions separate generation from selection: whether the pool holds at least one candidate that solves the task, and whether the selector can pick that candidate without accepting a broken one. A generator can flood the pool with proposals without making selection any easier; conversely, a sharper selector can raise end-to-end reliability without sampling a single extra candidate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Useful variation among candidates</h3>\n<p>Renaming local variables, reformatting comments, or rewriting a loop can produce eight distinct token sequences that all leave the synchronization bug untouched. Surface variation in token sequences does not guarantee diverse repair hypotheses. In the connection-pool repair, candidates add value only when they test different explanations that downstream checks can tell apart. Changing the thread-notification path vs. lengthening the timeout tests two distinct hypotheses. Generating half a dozen slightly reworded versions of the same timeout bump only wastes verification runs.</p>\n<p>This problem is separate from statistical dependence. When the task, prompt, base model, and sampling controls stay fixed, separate draws with independent random seeds are genuinely independent and identically distributed (IID). Even so, if the model puts almost all of its probability mass on timeout edits, independent draws will still fill the pool with variations on that one mistake.</p>\n<p>Across a mixed production workload, shared model blind spots matter even more. A model often solves familiar patterns reliably while failing every time on an unfamiliar edge case, so repeated samples on a hard task fail together far more often than the average pass rate across all tasks would suggest. While samples on a single fixed task may be conditionally independent, treating attempts across a mixed workload as draws with one shared pass probability hides that clustering. Feedback-driven revision changes the distribution again: once the runtime feeds test errors into later prompts, each new candidate depends directly on what earlier checks found.</p>\n<p>A runtime can encourage broader exploration by raising sampling temperature or prompting the model to try distinct diagnostic angles under the same task constraints. Pushing diversity too hard, however, often produces syntax errors, broken invariants, or nonsensical edits. A diversification method earns its keep only if it raises the task pass rate within a fixed compute and latency budget.</p>\n<p>To avoid wasting test runs on near-duplicates, the runtime can prune redundant candidates early by deduplicating on structure and behavior. Abstract syntax tree (AST) normalization strips away surface changes such as variable names and whitespace, and behavioral clustering runs candidates on cheap inputs to group those with identical outputs. Using both steps let a competitive code-generation system narrow tens of thousands of raw model samples down to a handful of distinct submissions [@li2022alphacode]. Each method groups candidates by a bounded equivalence relation, whether normalized syntax or matching outputs on a probe set, and deduplication is sound whenever that relation preserves the property the downstream check tests.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h3\">Choosing a selection rule</h3>\n<p>Picking the most common patch by majority vote fails whenever the model's favored mistake outnumbers the one candidate that fixes the bug, whereas running the full test suite on every raw sample wastes sandbox time on patches that do not even parse. Runtimes therefore combine three kinds of selection signal, ordered by cost and by the strength of the claim each supports: direct execution checks, answer agreement (consensus voting), and learned ranking.</p>\n<p>Direct checks, such as a parser, a type checker, or a test harness, test a candidate deterministically against explicit rules. Each establishes only the property it checks, as the scoped check pipeline of @sec-vol3-foundation-model-scoped-check-pipeline showed, so clean compilation says nothing about whether a race condition is gone. Running candidate code is also an external action that requires sandbox isolation, hard timeouts, and tight permission boundaries.</p>\n<p>Answer agreement combines independent reasoning paths by vote. In <strong>self-consistency</strong>, the runtime samples several reasoning chains, normalizes their final answers, and takes the plurality vote [@wang2022self]. That works well for arithmetic, symbolic logic, or short factual answers, but majority vote still reflects only what the generator produces most often. When a model shares a systematic bias or takes a flawed shortcut, independent samples regularly converge on the same wrong answer. For code repair, voting over raw patches is a poor selector: behavioral agreement inherits the coverage limits of the probe inputs, and a minority patch that actually passes the reproducer should beat a popular patch that fails it.</p>\n<p>When deterministic checks cannot cover every requirement, learned rankers and model-based judges supply a heuristic ordering. A learned reward model or a secondary large language model (LLM) judge (a model prompted to grade another model's output) can score explanation clarity, check style guidelines, or rank patches before the runtime spends time on heavy integration tests. Even so, ranking and acceptance answer different questions. Ranking orders candidates from best to worst under a score; acceptance decides whether a candidate meets the task contract. If every candidate in the pool is broken, picking the highest-ranked candidate returns the best-looking failure. Returning a candidate instead of abstaining requires a verifier that produces an objective pass or fail result.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Verifier-guided selection</strong></em> is a selection policy that returns a candidate only when a <strong>verifier</strong>, a check whose criteria and observations the candidate cannot modify, passes it, and abstains when no candidate passes.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Its success rate is capped by pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$, the chance the pool holds a passing candidate (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-pass-at-k), while the share of its selections that are correct depends on both the verifier's passing-set precision and the selection policy (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-check-precision). The fixed IID first-passing policy developed there preserves passing-set precision; ranking among passing candidates need not. Selection coverage and selected correctness together determine the probability of returning a valid result.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Voting selects what the generator produces most often and learned ranking selects what a scorer prefers; only a verifier's verdict can justify returning a candidate instead of abstaining.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Treating the verifier's pass as task acceptance; the verdict covers the checked property, and its false acceptance rate grows in importance as the pool's valid-candidate rate falls.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The hypothetical repair pool in @tbl-vol3-candidate-diversity shows what each check stage can and cannot establish. Here a focused reproducer exercises the known race condition, while full regression suites have not yet run.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Candidate</strong></th><th><strong>Proposed repair</strong></th><th><strong>Observed check result</strong></th><th><strong>Supported conclusion</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>A</td><td>Increase the timeout</td><td>Compiles; known failure remains</td><td>Structural validity is insufficient</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B</td><td>Retry after the timeout</td><td>Compiles; known failure remains</td><td>Different code can preserve the same defect</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C</td><td>Change notification handling with malformed syntax</td><td>Parsing fails</td><td>This artifact cannot proceed to execution testing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D</td><td>Change notification handling with valid syntax</td><td>Compiles; reproducer passes</td><td>Promising repair, with untested behavior still unresolved</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Evidence for candidate selection</strong>: A hypothetical repair pool separates the proposed mechanism, the observed result, and the conclusion each check supports. {#tbl-vol3-candidate-diversity tbl-colwidths=\"[12,25,30,33]\"}</p>\n<p>A production runtime stages these checks cheapest first, following the cost-ordered cascade of @tbl-verification-layers, so that parsing and AST checks drop malformed candidates before they reach sandboxed tests or an LLM judge. Inside a search, the stage order should come from measured run time and rejection rate rather than guesswork. Running a compiled binary in a warm container might take 5 ms, whereas calling a large LLM judge to grade code quality is a full model call that can take 800 ms and eat into the token budget. Comparing several candidates in a single prompt also scales differently with context length than scoring each candidate on its own. Because checking competes directly with generation for time and tokens, selection has to be budgeted as part of test-time compute.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">Candidate availability under pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$</h3>\n<p>Budgeting generation and selection separately starts when a runtime fails to return a working patch from a pool of $\\IndependentAttemptCount$ samples, because the engineer needs to know whether the generator never produced a passing patch or the selector threw a passing patch away. <strong>pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$</strong> separates those two failures by measuring the probability that at least one of $\\IndependentAttemptCount$ sampled candidates passes the specified tests [@chen2021evaluating].</p>\n<p>: <strong>Origin of pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$</strong>: The metric was introduced to score program synthesis by functional correctness rather than by textual similarity to a reference [@kulal2019spoc]. Because plugging the observed pass fraction $\\PassingSampleCount/\\GeneratedSampleCount$ into $1-(1-\\PassingSampleCount/\\GeneratedSampleCount)^\\IndependentAttemptCount$ biases the estimate of what breadth buys, @chen2021evaluating replaced it with the combinatorial estimator derived below.</p>\n<p>For a fixed task and fixed generation policy, suppose independent samples each pass the tests with probability $\\AttemptPassProbability$. The probability that all $\\IndependentAttemptCount$ samples fail is $(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^\\IndependentAttemptCount$, so</p>\n<p>$$ \\operatorname{pass@}\\IndependentAttemptCount = 1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^\\IndependentAttemptCount. $$</p>\n<p>This expression is conditional on that task and sampling policy. To summarize a workload with different task-level pass probabilities, average the task-level quantities; substituting the workload's average $\\AttemptPassProbability$ generally gives a different result. For an adaptive policy that revises its prompt after feedback, evaluate the policy's resulting pools rather than assuming they follow this fixed-distribution formula.</p>\n<p>In a benchmark, generate $\\GeneratedSampleCount \\ge \\IndependentAttemptCount$ candidates for a task and let $\\PassingSampleCount$ pass the fixed test criterion. Of the $\\binom{\\GeneratedSampleCount}{\\IndependentAttemptCount}$ subsets of size $\\IndependentAttemptCount$, exactly $\\binom{\\GeneratedSampleCount-\\PassingSampleCount}{\\IndependentAttemptCount}$ contain only failures. The subset estimator is therefore</p>\n<p>$$ \\PassAtKEstimate = 1-\\frac{\\binom{\\GeneratedSampleCount-\\PassingSampleCount}{\\IndependentAttemptCount}}{\\binom{\\GeneratedSampleCount}{\\IndependentAttemptCount}}, $$</p>\n<p>with the numerator taken as zero when $\\GeneratedSampleCount-\\PassingSampleCount&lt;\\IndependentAttemptCount$. Under independent, identically distributed sampling for the fixed task, this estimates the probability that a fresh pool of $\\IndependentAttemptCount$ candidates contains a test-passing member without bias; @sec-vol3-appendix-math-pass-at-k derives this expectation and details evaluation without factorials. Benchmark results average these estimates over tasks [@chen2021evaluating].</p>\n<p>The relationship to deployed selection follows from the event being measured. If the selector must return an unchanged member of the same pool, it cannot return a test-passing candidate when the pool contains none. Its probability of returning a test-passing candidate is therefore at most pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$, counting abstention as no returned pass. A selector that always finds a passing member whenever one exists reaches this ceiling; whether the deployed selector does, within the deployment's checking budget, is a separate measurement.</p>\n<p>The predicate in pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ is passing these tests, which is narrower than satisfying the complete task requirements. The ceiling also covers only selectors that return an unchanged pool member rather than repairing candidates or synthesizing a new artifact. Benchmarks therefore report candidate availability alongside selected-output performance under the relevant criterion, because the gap between the two can be large. @Fig-vol3-coverage-vs-selection recomputes the plateau described in @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-breadth-axis from the released per-sample correctness labels of @brown2024monkeys. It plots coverage (the task-averaged pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ estimate) against the accuracy of majority voting over the same pools.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ COVERAGE VS. SELECTED ACCURACY (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-vol3-coverage-vs-selection and the paragraph after it.\n# │ Goal: Quantify the gap between pool availability and what a voting\n# │       selector returns.\n# │ Show: Coverage and majority-vote accuracy at the largest pool; the\n# │       vote&#x27;s gain after 256 samples; repository-repair coverage growth.\n# │ How:  data/repeated_sampling_majority.csv / RepeatedSamplingCoverage,\n# │       recomputed from per-sample labels with the unbiased estimator.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import load_series\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check, fmt_int, fmt_percent\n\nclass CoverageVsSelection:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants) ──────────────────────────────────────────────\n    _maj_rows = load_series(&quot;vol3/03_test_time_compute/data/repeated_sampling_majority.csv&quot;).entries\n    _cov_rows = load_series(&quot;vol3/03_test_time_compute/data/repeated_sampling_coverage.csv&quot;).entries\n    _small_model = &quot;Llama-3-8B-Instruct&quot;\n    _large_model = &quot;Llama-3-70B-Instruct&quot;\n    _dataset = &quot;MATH&quot;\n    k_max, k_knee = 10_000, 256\n    _repair_system = &quot;DeepSeek-Coder-V2-Instruct + Moatless Tools&quot;\n    _repair_dataset = &quot;SWE-bench Lite (all 300)&quot;\n    repair_k = 250\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (The Compute) ────────────────────────────────────────\n    @staticmethod\n    def _at(rows, model, dataset, k, field):\n        return next(r[field] for r in rows if r[&quot;model&quot;] == model and r[&quot;dataset&quot;] == dataset and r[&quot;k&quot;] == k)\n\n    cov_small = _at(_maj_rows, _small_model, _dataset, k_max, &quot;coverage&quot;)\n    vote_small = _at(_maj_rows, _small_model, _dataset, k_max, &quot;majority_vote_accuracy&quot;)\n    vote_large = _at(_maj_rows, _large_model, _dataset, k_max, &quot;majority_vote_accuracy&quot;)\n    _gains = (\n        lambda fn=_at, mr=_maj_rows, ds=_dataset, km=k_max, kk=k_knee, sm=_small_model, lm=_large_model: [\n            fn(mr, m, ds, km, &quot;majority_vote_accuracy&quot;) - fn(mr, m, ds, kk, &quot;majority_vote_accuracy&quot;) for m in (sm, lm)\n        ]\n    )()\n    vote_gain_after_knee = max(0.0, *_gains)\n    cov_gain_after_knee = cov_small - _at(_maj_rows, _small_model, _dataset, k_knee, &quot;coverage&quot;)\n    repair_1 = _at(_cov_rows, _repair_system, _repair_dataset, 1, &quot;coverage&quot;)\n    repair_k_cov = _at(_cov_rows, _repair_system, _repair_dataset, repair_k, &quot;coverage&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(cov_small &gt; 0.95 and vote_small &lt; 0.5 and vote_large &lt; 0.65, &quot;Prose contrasts near-complete coverage with sub-60% voting&quot;)\n    check(vote_gain_after_knee &lt; 0.01 &lt; 0.05 &lt; cov_gain_after_knee, &quot;Prose says voting stalls after 256 samples while coverage keeps rising&quot;)\n    check(repair_k_cov &gt; 3 * repair_1, &quot;Prose says repository-repair coverage more than triples&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────\n    k_max_str, k_knee_str, repair_k_str = fmt_int(k_max), fmt_int(k_knee), fmt_int(repair_k)\n    cov_small_str = fmt_percent(round(cov_small, 2), precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    vote_small_str = fmt_percent(round(vote_small, 2), precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    vote_large_str = fmt_percent(round(vote_large, 2), precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    cov_gain_str = fmt_int(round(100 * cov_gain_after_knee))\n    repair_1_str = fmt_percent(round(repair_1, 2), precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    repair_cov_str = fmt_percent(round(repair_k_cov, 2), precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-coverage-vs-selection fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Availability Outruns Selection**: Coverage (solid), the share of problems whose pool holds a correct candidate, and majority-vote accuracy (dashed) on competition mathematics for two model sizes, against pool size on a logarithmic axis; the dotted line is coverage for a repository-repair agent whose candidates are checked by tests. Coverage keeps climbing for four orders of magnitude while voting flattens after a few hundred samples, so larger pools contain correct answers for more problems without a comparable gain in selected-output accuracy. Recomputed from the per-sample labels released by @brown2024monkeys.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Line chart of percent of problems from 0 to 100 against samples per problem from 1 to 10,000 on a logarithmic axis. Two solid lines, blue and violet, rise from about 27 and 48 percent to about 98 percent. Two dashed lines in the same colors rise briefly and flatten near 43 and 60 percent. A dotted green line rises from about 16 percent to 56 percent at 250 samples and stops.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n\nAt `[CoverageVsSelection.k_max_str]` samples, the smaller model&#x27;s pools hold a correct answer for `[CoverageVsSelection.cov_small_str]` of the problems, yet majority voting returns one for `[CoverageVsSelection.vote_small_str]`, and `[CoverageVsSelection.vote_large_str]` for the larger model. Beyond `[CoverageVsSelection.k_knee_str]` samples the vote gains less than a point while coverage gains another `[CoverageVsSelection.cov_gain_str]`. The repair agent&#x27;s coverage rises from `[CoverageVsSelection.repair_1_str]` with one sample to `[CoverageVsSelection.repair_cov_str]` at `[CoverageVsSelection.repair_k_str]`, but that growth is usable only because tests can identify which candidate passes. Spending on breadth is therefore justified by the selector, not by the pool. Without a check that recognizes the correct candidate, the added samples raise the pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ ceiling without raising what the runtime returns.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The distinction between pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ over a candidate pool and consistency over repeated system runs is developed in @sec-vol3-evaluation-pass-k.\n:::\n\n\n\nBreadth has a price that pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ makes easy to compute. In **best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$ sampling**, the runtime generates $\\CandidatePoolSize$ candidates for one decision and returns one that its selector admits. The pool is the same object pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ describes, so its availability is pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ with $\\IndependentAttemptCount = \\CandidatePoolSize$, written pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize$ from here on. The pool-pricing calculation compares that parallel pool against the simplest alternative, generating one candidate at a time and stopping at the first pass, under the most favorable selection assumption, in which the verifier is the acceptance check itself.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE PRICE OF A LARGER POOL (LEGO)</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: nbk-03-best-of-n-pool-price; fallacies (mixed-workload pass@N).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Compare parallel best-of-N with stop-at-first-pass sequential retry</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       at the same success probability.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: pass@N, marginal gain of the last candidate, candidates per success.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: IID candidates for a fixed task; the verifier is the acceptance check;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│      a perfect selector returns a passing member whenever one exists.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import *</p>\n<p>class BestOfN:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>scenario = Scenarios.IndependentCandidatePools p = scenario.pass_rate  # hypothetical per-candidate pass probability for a fixed task pools = tuple(scenario.pool_sizes)  # pool sizes compared p_easy = scenario.easy_pass_rate  # hypothetical mixed workload: half the tasks pass at this rate p_hard = scenario.hard_pass_rate  # hypothetical mixed workload: the other half never pass</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>result = scenario.result n_max = pools[-1] pass_at = list(result.pass_at.values())  # in pool order pass_max = pass_at[-1] last_gain = result.last_gain parallel_per_success = result.parallel_per_success seq_candidates = result.sequential_candidates  # expected attempts, stop at first pass, cap n_max seq_per_success = result.sequential_per_success  # reduces to 1 / p cost_ratio = result.cost_ratio p_mix = result.mixed_average_rate naive_mix = result.mixed_plug_in true_mix = result.mixed_true</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(pass_at == sorted(set(pass_at)), \"pass@N rises with N\") check(abs(seq_per_success - 1 / p) &lt; 1e-12, \"Sequential retry costs 1/p per success\") check(cost_ratio &gt; 2, \"Parallel pool costs over twice as much per success\") check(abs(p_mix - p) &lt; 1e-12, \"Mixed workload has the same average rate\") check(true_mix &lt; naive_mix - 0.3, \"Averaging p overstates mixed-workload pass@N\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>p_str = fmt_percent(p, precision=0, style=\"prose\") n_max_str = fmt_int(n_max) pools_str = MarkdownStr(\", \".join(str(n) for n in pools[:-1]) + \", and \" + str(pools[-1])) pass_1_str = fmt_int(round(100 <em> pass_at[0])) pass_2_str = fmt_int(round(100 </em> pass_at[1])) pass_4_str = fmt_int(round(100 <em> pass_at[2])) pass_max_str = fmt_percent(pass_max, precision=1, style=\"prose\") last_gain_pts_str = fmt(last_gain </em> 100, precision=1) parallel_per_success_str = fmt(parallel_per_success, precision=1) seq_candidates_str = fmt(seq_candidates, precision=2) seq_per_success_str = fmt(seq_per_success, precision=1) cost_mult_str = fmt_multiple(cost_ratio, precision=1) p_easy_str = fmt_percent(p_easy, precision=0, style=\"prose\") naive_mix_str = fmt_percent(naive_mix, precision=1, style=\"prose\") true_mix_str = fmt_percent(true_mix, precision=1, style=\"prose\") ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Each candidate repair passes the task's verifier with probability $\\AttemptPassProbability$, independently for this task, and the verifier is the acceptance check. How many candidates per accepted repair does a parallel pool of $\\CandidatePoolSize$ spend, compared with sequential retry that stops at the first pass and gives up after $\\CandidatePoolSize$ attempts?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>: $\\AttemptPassProbability$ = <code>[BestOfN.p_str]</code> (hypothetical); pools of <code>[BestOfN.pools_str]</code> candidates; a selector that returns a passing member whenever one exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: The formula pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize = 1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^\\CandidatePoolSize$ gives <code>[BestOfN.pass_1_str]</code>, <code>[BestOfN.pass_2_str]</code>, <code>[BestOfN.pass_4_str]</code>, and <code>[BestOfN.pass_max_str]</code> for those pools. The last of <code>[BestOfN.n_max_str]</code> candidates adds $\\AttemptPassProbability(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^{\\CandidatePoolSize-1}$, only <code>[BestOfN.last_gain_pts_str]</code> percentage points. The parallel pool always spends <code>[BestOfN.n_max_str]</code> candidates, or <code>[BestOfN.parallel_per_success_str]</code> per accepted repair. Sequential retry fails only when all <code>[BestOfN.n_max_str]</code> attempts fail, so it reaches the same <code>[BestOfN.pass_max_str]</code>. Because each subsequent attempt runs only when all earlier attempts failed (with probabilities $1, 1-\\AttemptPassProbability, \\dots, (1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^{\\CandidatePoolSize-1}$), its expected spend is the finite geometric sum $[1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^\\CandidatePoolSize]/\\AttemptPassProbability$ = <code>[BestOfN.seq_candidates_str]</code> candidates. Dividing that expected spend by the pass probability $1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^\\CandidatePoolSize$ cancels the numerator and leaves $1/\\AttemptPassProbability$ = <code>[BestOfN.seq_per_success_str]</code> candidates per accepted repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: At equal success, the parallel pool costs <code>[BestOfN.cost_mult_str]</code> as much per accepted repair; what it buys is one round instead of an expected <code>[BestOfN.seq_candidates_str]</code> sequential generate-and-check rounds. Breadth buys latency, not cost. In $\\CostPerAcceptedTask = \\mathbb{E}[\\AttemptCost]/\\AcceptanceProbability$ it raises $\\AcceptanceProbability$ per round but raises the attempt cost faster. Size $\\CandidatePoolSize$ as the smallest pool whose pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize$ meets the target within the rounds the deadline allows, and stop growing it once the next candidate's gain is worth less than its cost.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Balancing pool size against checking cost</h3>\n<p>Growing the candidate pool raises the chance that a valid repair enters the pool, but it also multiplies the checking load. Under a fixed deliberation budget, spending nearly everything on generation starves verification and forces the selector onto cheap, shallow heuristics. A smaller pool paired with a thorough check often beats a large pool paired with a weak selector; only when candidate generation itself is the bottleneck does a wider pool pay off right away. Comparing the two regimes therefore requires pricing generation and selection together rather than tuning either stage on its own.</p>\n<p>Checker errors also compound as the pool grows, because each extra invalid candidate gives an imperfect verifier another chance to register a false pass. If a verifier has a 5 percent false acceptance rate (it passes 5 percent of invalid candidates, defined formally in @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-check-precision), testing twenty invalid candidates with independent check outcomes makes at least one false pass more likely than not ($1 - (1 - 0.05)^{20} \\approx 64\\%$). Whether the selector actually returns that false pass depends on what else sits in the pool and on the tie-breaking rule, a distinction the next section makes exact. The defect rate of the returned candidate depends on the generator's base rate of valid candidates, the check's false-acceptance and false-rejection rates, and how the selector chooses among passing candidates. When an adaptive search steers generation using verifier scores, it shifts all three quantities at once and can push candidates straight into the verifier's blind spots.</p>\n<p>Selection rules and pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize$ price the candidate pool under a stated check, but they treat the check itself as a black box. Actual checks differ in whether they inspect finished artifacts or intermediate steps, how the generator's base rate bounds the precision of a passing verdict, and how quickly search pressure exploits their blind spots. Designing a selector that deserves trust therefore requires looking inside the verification checks themselves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h2\">Verifying Candidates</h2>\n<p>In a connection-pool repair, clean compilation confirms only syntax and types, and even a targeted concurrency test exercises only a handful of thread schedules and can miss a race condition. Every verification check answers a bounded question, never the whole question of whether a candidate is safe to ship. Engineers therefore have to decide which checks to run, whether to attach them to finished candidates or to intermediate steps, and how much trust to place in a passing verdict when valid candidates are rare.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h3\">Checking whole outputs vs. intermediate steps</h3>\n<p>Waiting until a candidate patch is completely written before running any check wastes every token spent elaborating a diagnosis that went wrong in step one, whereas scoring every intermediate step from scratch rescans the growing prefix again and again. An <strong>outcome check</strong> tests a completed candidate against an external rule, such as whether a patched service passes an end-to-end regression suite. An <strong>outcome reward model</strong>, by contrast, predicts a scalar quality score for the finished candidate, like the learned verifiers trained to rank candidate solutions in mathematical reasoning [@cobbe2021training]. The outcome check runs the artifact and reports what happened; the reward model only predicts how good the artifact looks.</p>\n<p>Step-level checks evaluate intermediate claims or partial states before the candidate is complete. In the connection-pool repair, a step check can test whether a waiting worker receives a wake-up signal before the agent drafts a full timeout patch. If the trace refutes the timeout explanation, the runtime drops that branch immediately and saves the remaining generation tokens. Learned process scorers similarly score or prune partial reasoning traces, using low step scores as early warnings of downstream failure. <strong>Process supervision</strong> attaches feedback to discrete intermediate steps rather than waiting for the final answer [@uesato2022solving; @lightman2023let]. What separates outcome feedback from process feedback is where the signal attaches, not what the scorer reads, since a learned outcome scorer can also read the full reasoning trace before grading the final output.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Step-Level Labels (PRM800K)</strong>: Outcome labels for code can be produced automatically by running tests. Step labels require a judgment on each intermediate step, and the PRM800K dataset used to train the process reward model of @lightman2023let contains about 800,000 human step-level labels. That labeling cost is one reason learned process scorers are scarcer than outcome checks.</p>\n<p>A <em><strong>process reward model</strong></em> (PRM) is a learned scorer that assigns a value to each intermediate step of a candidate trajectory, conditioned on the task context and the preceding steps.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Step scores allow search algorithms to prune unpromising branches early, keeping beam search and tree search, the search procedures that extend only the best-scoring partial candidates (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-search-structures), within finite computational budgets.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: An outcome reward model evaluates only terminal states; a process reward model assigns credit across intermediate steps. This early steering capability requires dense step-level supervision, which costs substantially more to gather than automated test outcomes.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: Mistaking a high step score for verified correctness. A process reward model provides heuristic guidance for ranking and pruning, but it offers no formal guarantees and satisfies no closure evidence level.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each granularity catches failures the other misses. An outcome check tests the assembled artifact end to end, catching integration bugs that look harmless inside any single step. A process check catches errors early and stops broken branches from burning more generation tokens, but checking too often adds scorer overhead and risks pruning good branches when step scores are noisy. Choosing how often to check balances saved generation tokens against scorer cost and false rejections.</p>\n<p>Step-scoring cost also depends on whether the serving engine reuses prefix state across steps. Suppose a scorer reads a task prompt of $\\SequenceLength$ tokens plus the candidate's growing prefix after each of its $\\ScoredStepCount$ steps, where each step adds $\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength$ tokens. Scoring each cumulative prefix from scratch, without reusing key-value cache state from earlier steps, processes</p>\n<p>$$ \\sum_{j=1}^{\\ScoredStepCount}\\left(\\SequenceLength + \\ScoredStepIndex\\,\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength\\right) = \\ScoredStepCount \\SequenceLength + \\frac{\\ScoredStepCount(\\ScoredStepCount+1)}{2}\\,\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength $$ {#eq-vol3-prm-rescoring}</p>\n<p>scorer input tokens, which grows quadratically with the number of steps $\\ScoredStepCount$. The beam-search calculation later in this chapter (notebook \\ref{nbk-03-compute-allocation-and-latency-trade-offs}) charges the $\\ScoredStepIndex\\,\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength$ term of @eq-vol3-prm-rescoring, with the task prompt $\\SequenceLength$ excluded, for every extension scored at step $\\ScoredStepIndex$. When the serving engine caches prefixes across steps, incremental scoring reads only the new $\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength$ tokens at each step and removes the quadratic growth; scorers that must re-read the whole candidate without prefix caching pay the full sum.</p>\n<p>@Tbl-vol3-verifier-classes compares four common kinds of check by what they inspect, where they can be fooled, whether they are deterministic, and which closure evidence level (@sec-vol3-intro-invariant-closure) they reach. Compilers and sealed test suites establish bounded, reproducible facts within their scope, whereas learned scorers only rank or prune candidates.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Check</strong></th><th><strong>Evaluates</strong></th><th><strong>Coverage</strong></th><th><strong>False-acceptance exposure</strong></th><th><strong>Deterministic</strong></th><th><strong>Closure evidence level</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Outcome scorer (learned)</td><td>Completed candidate</td><td>Any representation it was trained to read</td><td>Persuasive but wrong final text</td><td>No</td><td>None; ranks only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Process scorer (learned)</td><td>Intermediate step given its prefix</td><td>Steps it can delimit and judge</td><td>Locally plausible steps that drift from the task</td><td>No</td><td>None; ranks and prunes only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compiler or type checker</td><td>Syntax and declared types</td><td>Properties expressible in the type system</td><td>Behavior outside the type system</td><td>Yes</td><td>Static analysis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sealed test suite</td><td>Behavior on withheld cases</td><td>The cases and schedules it exercises</td><td>Untested inputs and schedules</td><td>Yes, under fixed conditions</td><td>Sealed tests, if the candidate cannot read or modify it</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Kinds of check and the claims they support</strong>: Learned scorers order candidates; compilers and sealed suites establish bounded properties. None dominates the others on every task, and a deterministic verdict remains incomplete outside its coverage. {#tbl-vol3-verifier-classes tbl-colwidths=\"[17,17,18,20,11,17]\"}</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h3\">How base rates limit check precision</h3>\n<p>When only 5 percent of generated patches actually fix a bug, even a test suite that falsely passes just 2 percent of broken patches leaves nearly a third of all passing candidates defective. The reliability question for any check is what share of the candidates that pass it are genuinely valid. Call meeting the full task specification $\\text{Valid}$ and receiving a positive check verdict $\\text{PASS}$. Let $\\ValidCandidateRate = \\Pr(\\text{Valid})$ be the base rate of valid candidates in the generator's output distribution, $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate = \\Pr(\\text{PASS}\\mid\\neg\\text{Valid})$ the <strong>false acceptance rate</strong>, and $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate = \\Pr(\\neg\\text{PASS}\\mid\\text{Valid})$ the <strong>false rejection rate</strong>. Here \"acceptance\" means passing the internal verifier, not authorization to ship to production. The valid-candidate rate $\\ValidCandidateRate$ is not the test-pass probability $\\AttemptPassProbability$ used in pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$: $\\ValidCandidateRate$ is the chance of meeting the full task specification, whereas $\\AttemptPassProbability$ is the chance of passing the stated tests. A perfect check makes the two coincide; an imperfect check separates them, and the precision formula below measures the gap.</p>\n<p>The total probability that a random candidate passes the verifier is $\\VerifierPassProbability=\\ValidCandidateRate(1-\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate)+(1-\\ValidCandidateRate)\\,\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate$. Applying the same Bayes' rule that gave the defect share among one checker's passing patches (@eq-vol3-fm-checker-posterior) gives the precision of positive verdicts:</p>\n<p>$$ \\VerifierPrecision \\equiv \\Pr(\\text{Valid}\\mid\\text{PASS}) =\\frac{\\ValidCandidateRate(1-\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate)}{\\ValidCandidateRate(1-\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate)+(1-\\ValidCandidateRate)\\,\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate}. $$ {#eq-vol3-verifier-precision}</p>\n<p>When valid candidates are rare, even a check with a small false acceptance rate produces a passing pool where broken candidates make up a large share, the same base-rate trap that @sec-vol3-foundation-model-self-review-evidence showed for a single checked patch. Applied across a candidate pool, this is the verification asymmetry (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-verification-asymmetry}) in quantitative form. Generating a passing candidate and knowing that a passing candidate is valid are different events, and the base rate and checker error rates, not the pool size, bound how far a pass can be trusted. Both error rates therefore have to be measured on real tasks for each model family and test harness.</p>\n<p>While @eq-vol3-verifier-precision describes the pool of passing candidates as a whole, the reliability of the single candidate the runtime <em>returns</em> depends on the selection rule. Suppose candidate-check pairs are drawn independently and identically for a fixed task under constant error rates. Suppose also that the runtime checks the pool in a fixed order unrelated to quality, returns the first candidate that passes, and abstains if none passes. Under that first-passing rule, the probability that a returned candidate is valid equals $\\VerifierPrecision$: learning that earlier candidates in the list failed does not change the conditional validity of the first one that passes. Picking the candidate with the highest continuous reward score behaves differently, because maximizing a noisy score actively concentrates positive scoring errors, as the next subsection shows.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A repair policy generates all <code>[CheckYield.n_str]</code> candidates before selection. It uses the first-passing selection rule and abstains when no candidate passes. What does additional breadth buy, and what risk remains?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>: All rates are hypothetical. Each candidate contains <code>[CheckYield.tokens_str]</code> tokens, billed at <code>[CheckYield.price_str]</code> per million output tokens. The valid-candidate rate is <code>[CheckYield.p_str]</code>, false rejection is <code>[CheckYield.eps_fr_str]</code>, and false acceptance is <code>[CheckYield.eps_fa_str]</code>. Suppose a separately authorized deployment policy deploys the selected candidate without further checking. A defective deployment incurs <code>[CheckYield.loss_str]</code> in incident loss. For this calculation alone, valid deployments and abstentions incur zero incident loss, and missed repairs and delay are left unpriced.</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: The expected passing counts are <code>[CheckYield.valid_passes_str]</code> valid candidates and <code>[CheckYield.invalid_passes_str]</code> invalid candidates, totaling <code>[CheckYield.passes_str]</code>. @Eq-vol3-verifier-precision yields <code>[CheckYield.precision_str]</code> precision. Conditional on making a selection, the probability of a defective selection is therefore <code>[CheckYield.defect_str]</code>, with expected incident loss <code>[CheckYield.conditional_loss_str]</code> per selected deployment.</p>\n<p>The probability of any selection is $1-(1-\\VerifierPassProbability)^\\CandidatePoolSize$, or <code>[CheckYield.coverage_str]</code>; the policy abstains on <code>[CheckYield.abstention_str]</code> of batches. Multiplying selection coverage by conditional defect probability gives <code>[CheckYield.wrong_str]</code> defective selections per task attempt and <code>[CheckYield.task_loss_str]</code> expected incident loss per attempt, including abstentions. Generating the entire batch costs <code>[CheckYield.bill_str]</code> in output charges. Input charges, checking, and other operational costs are omitted. A sequential policy that stops generation at the first pass would have a different generation bill.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Expected incident loss per attempt is about <code>[CheckYield.loss_to_bill_str]</code> times the output bill, so the acceptance policy, not the token price, sets the cost of this task. Precision responds to two knobs of similar strength: halving the false acceptance rate raises it from <code>[CheckYield.precision_str]</code> to <code>[CheckYield.precision_half_fa_str]</code>, and doubling the valid-candidate rate raises it to <code>[CheckYield.precision_double_p_str]</code>. More candidates move neither. At a low base rate, spend on whichever of a better generator or a stricter check is cheaper, and price false rejections too when a missed valid repair is costly.</p>\n<p>With $\\ValidCandidateRate$, $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate$, and $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate$ held fixed, growing the pool size $\\CandidatePoolSize$ does not change precision $\\VerifierPrecision$. A wider pool raises the chance of finding at least one passing candidate, but it also raises the chance that the batch holds at least one false pass: $1-[1-(1-\\ValidCandidateRate)\\,\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate]^\\CandidatePoolSize$. In the repair-pool calculation, that false-pass exposure reaches <code>[CheckYield.any_false_str]</code>, while the probability of actually returning a defective candidate is <code>[CheckYield.wrong_str]</code>. The gap arises because under a first-passing rule a false pass later in the batch does no harm if an earlier valid candidate passes first. Pool availability, passing-set precision, and deployed defect risk are three distinct numbers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h3\">How search exploits imperfect checks</h3>\n<p>The more candidates a search generates and ranks against an imperfect scorer or test suite, the more likely the winning candidate is one that exploited a scorer blind spot or deleted a failing assertion rather than fixing the bug. Fixed error rates describe passive sampling from a fixed distribution, but adaptive search changes the candidates themselves. When a search policy steers generation or picks the maximum of a noisy score, it pushes candidates into regions where the evaluator is poorly calibrated, so the baseline rates $\\ValidCandidateRate$, $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate$, and $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate$ no longer hold for the candidates that reach the end of the search. Optimizing against an imperfect proxy breaks down in three distinct ways [@manheim2018categorizing]. Manheim and Garrabrant classify them as forms of Goodhart's law. In <em>regressional</em> Goodhart, picking the highest score selects for positive measurement noise. In <em>extremal</em> Goodhart, correlations that hold on typical candidates break down in the tail reached by search. In <em>adversarial</em> Goodhart, candidate code tampers with or bypasses the test harness.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Goodhart's Law</strong>: The economist Charles Goodhart stated it in 1975 about monetary policy: any observed statistical regularity tends to collapse once pressure is placed upon it for control purposes [@goodhart1984]. The anthropologist Marilyn Strathern later gave it the form usually quoted today, that when a measure becomes a target it ceases to be a good measure [@strathern1997improving]. A test suite used to select patches is such a regularity, a reliable description of past candidates that weakens once search optimizes against it.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ SELECTION ON A NOISY SCORE (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-selection-pressure.\n# │ Goal: Show how argmax selection concentrates positive scoring error, and\n# │       how a rare checker gap becomes likely in a large pool.\n# │ Show: Exact expected maximum of N standard normal errors; exploit presence.\n# │ How: Numerical integration of N x phi(x) Phi(x)^(N-1); IID candidates.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\n\nclass ArgmaxSelection:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    scenario = Scenarios.ArgmaxScoreNoise\n    n_small = scenario.small_pool  # hypothetical small pool\n    n_large = scenario.large_pool  # same batch size as the yield notebook\n    gap_rate = scenario.exploit_rate  # hypothetical fraction of candidates exploiting a check gap\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    result = scenario.result  # exact E[max] of N standard normals, by numerical integration\n    shift_small = result.expected_max[n_small]\n    shift_large = result.expected_max[n_large]\n    asym_large = result.asymptote[n_large]\n    gap_present = result.exploit_presence\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(result.expected_max[1]) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Mean of one standard normal is zero&quot;)\n    check(0 &lt; shift_small &lt; shift_large &lt; asym_large, &quot;Selected error grows with N, below the asymptote&quot;)\n    check(0 &lt; gap_present &lt; 1, &quot;Probability bounds&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    n_small_str = fmt_int(n_small)\n    n_large_str = fmt_int(n_large)\n    shift_small_str = fmt(shift_small, precision=1)\n    shift_large_str = fmt(shift_large, precision=1)\n    asym_large_str = fmt(asym_large, precision=1)\n    gap_rate_str = fmt_percent(gap_rate, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    gap_present_str = fmt_percent(gap_present, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\nThe regressional effect can be written out directly. Suppose a learned scorer reports an estimated utility $\\EstimatedCandidateUtility_i = \\CandidateUtility_i + \\CandidateScoringError_i$ for candidate $\\CandidateIndex$, where $\\CandidateUtility_i$ is true task utility and $\\CandidateScoringError_i$ is an independent scoring error with mean zero and standard deviation $\\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation$ (scoring noise, distinct from the per-turn error rate in horizon compounding). Picking $\\arg\\max_i \\EstimatedCandidateUtility_i$ maximizes the sum of utility and noise, so the winning candidate is disproportionately one that drew a large positive error. For independent Gaussian errors, the expected maximum error across $\\CandidatePoolSize$ candidates scales for large $\\CandidatePoolSize$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}\\Bigl[\\max_{i \\le \\CandidatePoolSize} \\CandidateScoringError_i\\Bigr] \\approx \\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation\\sqrt{2\\ln \\CandidatePoolSize}.\n$$ {#eq-vol3-argmax-noise}\n\nIn units of $\\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation$, the exact expectation is `[ArgmaxSelection.shift_small_str]` at `[ArgmaxSelection.n_small_str]` candidates and `[ArgmaxSelection.shift_large_str]` at `[ArgmaxSelection.n_large_str]` candidates, where the asymptotic formula gives `[ArgmaxSelection.asym_large_str]`. That expected maximum is an upper bound on the winner&#x27;s expected scoring error, reached when all candidates have the same true utility. When true utilities vary, selection responds to both utility and noise (Exercise 8 derives the winner&#x27;s error for a Gaussian utility model). An acceptance threshold calibrated on single, unselected completions therefore cannot be reused unmodified on argmax-selected winners.\n\n: **Extreme-Value Asymptotics**: The form $\\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation\\sqrt{2\\ln \\CandidatePoolSize}$ is only the leading term of the Gaussian maximum, and convergence is slow, which is why the chapter computes the exact expectation beside it. The Gaussian is also a favorable case. Scoring errors with heavier tails, such as a learned judge that occasionally misjudges badly, push the selected error up faster than $\\sqrt{\\ln \\CandidatePoolSize}$, so the drift in a real pool can exceed this estimate. \n\nA rising scoring error on the winner does not by itself mean that true utility is falling. In Exercise 8(b), independent Gaussian utility and noise make both the winner&#x27;s expected utility and its expected scoring error rise with pool size. Empirical studies of best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$ sampling under learned reward models show a sharper divergence: the proxy score keeps climbing while a held-out reference score rises, flattens, and then drops [@gao2023scaling]. That downturn comes from tail miscalibration or exploitable gaps in the proxy, not from @eq-vol3-argmax-noise alone. @Fig-vol3-goodhart-divergence sketches the pattern, with the winner&#x27;s proxy score rising (dashed blue curve) while the held-out reference score peaks and turns down (solid green curve).\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-goodhart-divergence fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Proxy and Reference Scores under Selection**: A qualitative sketch of the measured best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$ pattern. The proxy score of the selected candidate keeps rising as $\\CandidatePoolSize$ grows, while a held-out reference score rises and then declines. The widening gap illustrates proxy optimism under selection; the declining reference score is an empirical pattern, not a necessary consequence of growing scoring error. The axes carry no values, and the point where the reference score turns down depends on the task.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Line sketch with candidates selected from, N, on a log-scale horizontal axis and score of the selected candidate on the vertical axis, without tick values. A dashed blue curve labeled proxy score rises and flattens. A solid green curve labeled held-out reference follows it at first, peaks near the middle at a marked dot, and then declines. The region between the curves is shaded, a red double-headed arrow at the right labels the gap scoring error of the winner, and a dashed line below the green peak notes that where the decline begins depends on the task.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nChecker blind spots compound across large pools by the same binomial arithmetic as pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$. If an unmodeled edge case or loophole appears in a fraction $\\ExploitCandidateFraction$ of single draws, a pool of $\\CandidatePoolSize$ candidates contains at least one loophole-exploiting candidate with probability $1-(1-\\ExploitCandidateFraction)^\\CandidatePoolSize$. A loophole that shows up in only `[ArgmaxSelection.gap_rate_str]` of individual samples, rare enough to miss during single-call testing, appears in a batch of `[ArgmaxSelection.n_large_str]` candidates with probability `[ArgmaxSelection.gap_present_str]`. Once a broken candidate slips through that loophole and receives a passing mark, competitive selection promotes it. Checker accuracy measured on unselected outputs therefore overstates accuracy under search, and the gap widens as the pool grows.\n\nIn the connection-pool repair, a patch can pass the test suite either by fixing the missing wake-up notification or by deleting the unit-test assertion that checks for blocked worker threads. Both edits raise the test score, and only the first fixes the bug. The candidate under test must therefore be isolated from the verifier so that generated code cannot edit test files or forge pass signals.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Grader isolation enforces this separation down to the candidate&#x27;s process and interpreter, as developed in @sec-vol3-evaluation-gyms-grader-isolation.\n:::\n\nIsolating the grader stops tampering, but it does not widen what the test suite covers: a sealed regression suite still misses untested thread schedules, and a learned judge can still prefer confident, broken code. Evaluation must therefore test the outputs that the complete search policy produces at its deployed budget, counting false acceptances and abstentions explicitly. Adding confidence requires an independent check that covers a different failure mode rather than a second correlated scorer.\n\nVerification also produces concrete intermediate facts that later turns can reuse, including refuted diagnoses, verified invariants, and passing test subsets. When a check refutes one assumption inside a multi-part candidate plan, the runtime does not need to throw away components that never depended on that assumption, provided it records which parts of the plan rest on which premises.\n\n## Revising Candidate Plans {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-planning-revision}\n\nWhen a targeted test refutes the initial diagnosis inside a multi-part repair plan, throwing away the entire plan wastes valid work such as API compatibility checks, whereas keeping the plan unchanged leaves the patch and test selection resting on a disproven premise. Suppose the repair agent forms a diagnosis, drafts a patch, checks public API constraints, and picks a test plan for a connection-pool leak, and a targeted test then refutes the diagnosis. To avoid both waste and stale conclusions, the runtime needs to identify which parts of the candidate depend on the refuted premise before deciding what the next model call should regenerate.\n\nA **revisable candidate plan** records those dependencies explicitly. Instead of logging past actions, it tracks unexecuted candidate artifacts together with the evidence or upstream artifacts each one relies on. When new evidence refutes a premise, the runtime invalidates and regenerates only the affected subgraph while keeping supported artifacts intact, and the replacement artifacts then go through their own checks. This design comes directly from truth maintenance systems, which record explicit justifications for each conclusion so that retracting a premise automatically invalidates every step built on it [@doyle1979truth].\n\n### Tracking dependencies inside a candidate {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-plan-graph}\n\nSuppose the first repair candidate blames the connection-pool failure on an overly short client timeout. To know which parts of that candidate survive when a test refutes the timeout hypothesis, the runtime represents the candidate as a directed acyclic graph with a directed edge from each prerequisite to the artifact that depends on it (@tbl-vol3-candidate-plan-dependencies). Here the source excerpt, initial failure log, API declaration, and test inventory are supplied inputs, whereas the diagnosis, patch candidate, check selection, and API compatibility notes are generated artifacts. The graph records which components depend on which, whether or not the model generated them in that order.\n\n| Artifact                | Direct prerequisites                        | What the artifact contributes                                     |\n|:------------------------|:--------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Source excerpt          | Repository revision and file identity       | The implementation being considered                               |\n| Diagnosis               | Source excerpt and failure observation      | A proposed explanation of the failure                             |\n| Patch candidate         | Source excerpt and diagnosis                | A proposed change to that implementation                          |\n| Test inventory          | Repository revision (a supplied input)      | The tests available for selection                                 |\n| Check selection         | Patch candidate and test inventory          | Checks intended to distinguish this repair from an inadequate one |\n| API compatibility notes | Task constraints and public API declaration | Restrictions that every acceptable repair must preserve           |\n: **Dependencies within a repair candidate**: Each row names the inputs needed to interpret its artifact. A separate compatibility branch can be retained only while its own inputs remain applicable. {#tbl-vol3-candidate-plan-dependencies}\n\nNow suppose the waiter-and-return test shows that an idle connection entered the pool while a waiting worker stayed blocked past the notification point. That observation refutes the timeout diagnosis and points to a missed wake-up notification instead. Because both the timeout patch and its check selection depend on the refuted diagnosis, the runtime marks them invalid and schedules them for replacement. The source excerpt stays valid because the repository file has not changed, and the API compatibility notes stay valid because the public interface constraints have not changed. The runtime builds the next prompt from the still-valid inputs plus the new test observation, asks for a new diagnosis, patch, and check selection, and runs checks on the new artifacts.\n\nThat selective reuse is only as sound as the recorded edges. A graph with a missing edge keeps stale conclusions just as an unmanaged prompt buffer does: if the compatibility notes silently depended on helper signatures that the new patch changes, leaving out that edge keeps broken compatibility claims in place. Dependency tracking pays off when maintaining the graph and walking its edges costs less in time and tokens than regenerating the unaffected branches from scratch.\n\n### Invalidating stale and refuted evidence {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-precondition-invalidation}\n\nWalking the dependency graph requires distinguishing an actively refuted claim from an observation whose preconditions no longer match the current candidate. A failing test directly refutes the claim that a patch fixes the bug, whereas a passing test from an earlier candidate remains a true record of what that earlier patch did while saying nothing about the edited patch now on the table. Reusing the earlier pass after the code changes would let an unverified edit slip through on stale evidence, whereas treating an expired observation as a direct contradiction would falsely rule out a working hypothesis.\n\nTo keep reuse sound, every recorded observation must carry its full provenance: the exact source revision or patch digest, test suite version, compiler flags, and runtime environment. Likewise, an observation from reading a file must bind to the cryptographic hash of the file contents when read, not to a file path whose contents a later step might overwrite.\n\nWhen an input changes or a premise fails, the runtime marks that node unsupported and walks the directed edges to find every downstream artifact that needs review. If node $\\InvalidatedArtifactNode$ loses support, the invalidated set is its reachable descendants in the graph: $\\text{Desc}(\\InvalidatedArtifactNode) = \\{\\ReachableArtifactNode \\mid \\InvalidatedArtifactNode \\rightsquigarrow \\ReachableArtifactNode\\}$, where $\\InvalidatedArtifactNode \\rightsquigarrow \\ReachableArtifactNode$ means a directed path runs from $\\InvalidatedArtifactNode$ to $\\ReachableArtifactNode$. Breadth-first search finds $\\text{Desc}(\\InvalidatedArtifactNode)$ in time linear in the vertices and edges. Every node outside $\\text{Desc}(\\InvalidatedArtifactNode)$ keeps its support, including separate subgraphs such as the API compatibility notes. Walking a candidate graph of dozens or hundreds of nodes, like the one in @tbl-vol3-candidate-plan-dependencies, takes a negligible fraction of the illustrative `[ProbeAllocation.token_time_str]` needed to decode a single token. The real engineering work is therefore recording the dependency edges accurately rather than traversing them. In the connection-pool repair, the waiter-and-return test contradicts the timeout diagnosis, and graph traversal marks its descendants (the patch and check selection) for review. The next call regenerates only the diagnosis and that branch from the retained inputs (@fig-vol3-plan-revision).\n\n: **Dependency-Driven Rebuilding**: Build tools solved the same problem for files. Make rebuilds a target only when one of its declared prerequisites is newer than the target [@feldman1979make], and its characteristic failure is the one described here, in which an undeclared dependency leaves a stale target that looks up to date. The saving is only as sound as the recorded edges. \n\n::: {#fig-vol3-plan-revision fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Invalidation and Scoped Regeneration**: In (A), the waiter-and-return test contradicts the timeout diagnosis. Following the recorded dependency edges marks the patch and check selection for review, while the recorded inputs and the separately derived API notes keep their support. In (B), a new request supplies that supported frontier and regenerates only the diagnosis, patch, and check selection. The graph records candidate artifacts, not executed operations, and each replacement must still pass its own checks. The test inventory that also feeds check selection is omitted.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two stacked panels with the same dependency graph. Inputs on the left are source excerpt, failure observation, waiter-return test, and API declaration. In panel A, source excerpt and failure observation feed a red diagnosis marked timeout too short, which feeds a patch candidate and then a check selection; source excerpt also feeds the patch candidate. The patch candidate and check selection are drawn with dashed red borders, labeled needs review, inside a dashed region labeled Desc of diagnosis. The waiter-return test points to the diagnosis with a dashed red arrow labeled contradicts. The API declaration feeds API notes labeled support kept. In panel B, a dashed blue region labeled regenerated contains diagnosis prime labeled missed notification, patch candidate prime labeled notify on return, and check selection prime labeled ordering test; the same inputs, now including the waiter-return test, feed diagnosis prime, and API notes are labeled retained.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nContent hashes and environment digests make invalidation deterministic only when the recorded boundary captures every input that matters. An integration test that calls a live network service can change its outcome even when local files stay bit-identical, whereas editing an unrelated README file should not invalidate unit-test results whose code and inputs did not change. Sound reuse therefore depends on checking the full execution context (sandbox isolation, mocks, and declared inputs) rather than file timestamps or paths alone.\n\nCandidate plans must also be kept separate from live environment state. The dependency graph tracks uncommitted candidate artifacts and their justifications; reading files, writing to disk, or running binaries in a sandbox changes real system state. Dropping an invalidated node from the plan graph does not roll back a modified file or free a running container, so the runtime must handle rollback and cleanup through separate recovery routines.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Compensation and cleanup of external effects that outlive an abandoned candidate branch are developed in @sec-vol3-failure-recovery-branch-compensation.\n:::\n\n### Choosing how much of a plan to regenerate {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-revision-mechanics}\n\nOnce graph traversal marks the invalidated nodes, the runtime must decide whether to replace a single node, rebuild its entire downstream subgraph, or stop deliberating altogether. Replacing a single step in isolation fails when the new artifact changes an interface that downstream steps rely on, whereas regenerating the whole plan from scratch wastes tokens and risks breaking parts that already worked. To scope a revision cleanly, the runtime builds a prompt with the task constraints, the still-valid upstream frontier, the observation that refuted the old step, and the exact nodes marked for replacement. In the connection-pool repair, the prompt asks for a new diagnosis, patch, and check selection while keeping the unchanged source excerpt and API notes. When the model returns replacement artifacts, the runtime checks both that they fit the retained artifacts and that their own prerequisites are met, because a locally valid replacement can still clash with preserved downstream nodes.\n\nDepending on how much support the new observation removes, the runtime chooses among three responses. @Fig-vol3-hierarchical-revision orders these three responses around two questions, whether the dependents of the affected artifact still have their needs met and whether the task&#x27;s own premises still hold:\n\n1. A **local repair** replaces a single node while keeping its interface and contracts unchanged so that its dependents stay valid, such as fixing a malformed shell flag in a test command without changing what the test checks.\n2. A **subgraph resynthesis** replaces a node and all of its descendants, feeding the model the intact upstream frontier and asking it to rebuild the path to the goal. In the connection-pool repair, this replaces the diagnosis, patch, and test selection while keeping the source excerpt and API compatibility notes.\n3. **Stopping deliberation** applies when new evidence refutes a premise of the task itself, such as a missing host library or credentials without write access. No plan revision can fix a broken task premise, so the runtime stops deliberating and escalates or reports the task unresolved instead of spending more tokens.\n\n::: {#fig-vol3-hierarchical-revision fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;htb&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Choosing the Revision Scope**: Two questions about the new evidence select among three responses. If the artifact&#x27;s dependents still have what they need, a local repair replaces it alone. If they do not but the task&#x27;s premises hold, subgraph resynthesis replaces the artifact and its descendants. If a premise has failed, deliberation stops and the task is reported unresolved or escalated. The tiers classify responses by how much support the evidence removes rather than establishing an unconditional preference for minimal edits.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Flowchart. An amber box reading new evidence removes support from an artifact leads to a diamond asking whether dependents&#x27; needs are still met. Yes leads down to a card labeled local repair. No leads right to a second diamond asking whether task premises still hold. Yes leads down to a card labeled subgraph resynthesis. No leads by a red arrow to a red card labeled stop deliberation. Each card draws the same small dependency graph, a premise square feeding a chain of three artifact nodes and one side node. In local repair only the first chain node is filled and outlined as replaced, with the caption replace one artifact. In subgraph resynthesis the whole chain is filled and outlined, with the caption replace artifact plus descendants. In stop deliberation the premise square is red and every node is dashed, with the caption report unresolved or escalate. An arrow along the bottom runs from less support removed to more support removed.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nSmallest is not always best. When new evidence overturns an early framing assumption or the dependency graph has grown tangled, regenerating the whole candidate from scratch often produces a cleaner, more consistent result than patching a large subgraph, at the cost of re-emitting settled material and risking regressions in parts that already worked. Choosing between scoped revision and full regeneration weighs the share of the graph that was invalidated against the tokens, time, and checks needed to rebuild and verify the candidate.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ CANDIDATE PLAN REVISION OUTPUT</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-revision-mechanics,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          nbk-03-compute-and-token-savings-of.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Compare output tokens for full and scoped candidate regeneration.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Generated token counts, retained output share, and avoided output.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Hypothetical equal-sized nodes; only replaced nodes are generated.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Note: Excludes input processing, checks, scheduling, and physical effects.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import check</p>\n<p>class PlanRevision:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>scenario = Scenarios.ScopedPlanRevision total_nodes = scenario.total_nodes  # hypothetical complete candidate size replacement_nodes = scenario.replaced_nodes  # hypothetical affected subset tokens_per_node = scenario.tokens_per_node * count  # hypothetical generated tokens per node</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>result = scenario.result full_output = result.full_output <em> count local_output = result.scoped_output </em> count output_share = result.output_share avoided_share = result.avoided_share</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(0 &lt; replacement_nodes &lt;= total_nodes, \"replacement nodes within total\") check(local_output &lt;= full_output, \"local output &lt;= full output\") check(0 &lt;= avoided_share &lt; 1, \"avoided share in [0, 1)\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. EXPORT (Narrative Values) ─────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>total_nodes_str = fmt_count(total_nodes) replacement_nodes_str = fmt_count(replacement_nodes) tokens_per_node_str = fmt_tokens(tokens_per_node) full_output_str = fmt_tokens(full_output) local_output_str = fmt_tokens(local_output) output_share_str = fmt_percent(output_share, style=\"prose\") avoided_share_str = fmt_percent(avoided_share, style=\"prose\") ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Suppose a candidate contains <code>[PlanRevision.total_nodes_str]</code> nodes, each requiring <code>[PlanRevision.tokens_per_node_str]</code> generated tokens. New evidence leaves <code>[PlanRevision.replacement_nodes_str]</code> nodes needing replacement. How much output does scoped regeneration avoid if either approach produces a candidate adequate for the same subsequent checks?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Multiplying the complete node count by the output per node gives <code>[PlanRevision.full_output_str]</code> tokens for full regeneration. Multiplying the replacement count by the same output allowance gives <code>[PlanRevision.local_output_str]</code> tokens for scoped regeneration. The scoped request generates <code>[PlanRevision.output_share_str]</code> of the full request's output, avoiding <code>[PlanRevision.avoided_share_str]</code> of that output under the equal-node-size assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Scoped regeneration saves the exact fraction of the plan that keeps its support. That saving shrinks linearly as the refuted premise reaches more descendants and disappears when every node depends on it, so once most of the plan rests on the rejected premise, full regeneration costs little more and is simpler to check. This count covers generated plan tokens only; both options still pay for prompt input, integration, and verification checks.</p>\n<p>These output savings have to be weighed against the work of maintaining dependencies, building scoped prompts, and checking hybrid plans. Once a candidate plan passes verification and crosses into execution, it stops being an uncommitted candidate graph and becomes live workflow state that the harness executes and durable execution checkpoints across machine failures.</p>\n<p>↪ Persisting committed plan state across worker and infrastructure faults is developed in @sec-vol3-durable-execution.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h3\">Preventing revision thrashing</h3>\n<p>Even when each scoped revision saves tokens compared with full regeneration, a revision loop without stopping rules can still oscillate between two diagnoses, produce cosmetically different patches with the same bug, or regenerate plans in response to harmless compiler warnings. The runtime therefore checks whether a proposed revision responds to new evidence that actually refutes a premise, rather than treating every warning or score fluctuation as a reason to replan.</p>\n<p>To prevent thrashing, the runtime enforces hard ceilings on model calls, total tokens, check runs, and wall-clock time. Every revision attempt, including a failed local repair, draws from that allowance, and the same ceilings bound the search as a whole (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-early-exit). When the budget runs out, the loop stops and reports the task unresolved or escalates to a human operator without merging the unverified patch. Seeing the same or structurally equivalent candidate under unchanged observations also triggers an immediate stop; revisiting an earlier idea is justified only when a new test observation has changed the evidence.</p>\n<p>Dependency graphs let the runtime revise a single candidate without throwing away its valid parts, but an agent often holds several partial candidates at once. Deciding which partial candidate to extend next, how to keep concurrent branches from corrupting one another's workspaces, and when to stop searching altogether turns revision into bounded search.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-15\" class=\"cs-h2\">Bounded Search</h2>\n<p>The repair agent now holds several partial candidates at once, some freshly revised and some untouched. At each step it can sample a fresh patch from the root, extend an existing partial candidate by one segment, or run a diagnostic probe to separate competing explanations. Every choice draws from the same task budget while creating different scheduling dependencies. A probe that gates the next code extension sits on the critical path. Independent candidate extensions can run in parallel across worker slots. Dropping a dead-end branch still forfeits the tokens and sandbox time already spent on it. A search policy therefore needs a pruning structure, workspace isolation between branches, separate accounts for tokens, time, and memory, reservations for in-flight work, and an ordered stopping rule.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h3\">Where each search structure prunes</h3>\n<p>Decoding a candidate patch to full length after its first line already rests on a broken premise wastes every remaining token on that branch, yet checking after every short segment stalls generation behind a scorer. Search structures trade those two costs by choosing when to check and discard partial candidates. Flat sampling waits until the end: in best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$, the runtime dispatches up to $\\CandidatePoolSize$ independent generation calls and runs its selector only after all of them finish. Because the calls do not depend on one another, free serving capacity turns directly into lower latency through batching, provided the cluster can run all $\\CandidatePoolSize$ streams without slowing per-token decoding, at the price of decoding every flawed branch to completion.</p>\n<p><strong>Beam search</strong> moves that check inside the generation loop. The runtime keeps a frontier of at most $\\BeamWidth$ partial candidates (the beam width). At each step it generates a fixed number of extensions per retained candidate, scores the extended prefixes, and keeps only the top $\\BeamWidth$ survivors for the next step. If fewer than $\\BeamWidth$ survive a check, only those survivors advance, and the root starts as a single prompt. Step scores can combine model log-probabilities, a learned process scorer, or a deterministic check. While $\\BeamWidth$ caps the survivors carried between stages, peak memory during expansion must hold $\\BeamWidth$ times the branching factor in active extensions along with their KV caches, scores, and parent pointers.</p>\n<p>Pruning at each step trades generation tokens for scoring work and bets on the accuracy of the step scorer. If a noisy step score drops a prefix that would have led to a working patch, a fixed-width beam search never visits that branch again unless backtracking is added. Conversely, killing a broken prefix early saves all the tokens and final test runs its completion would have cost. Step pruning pays off when scoring costs less than the avoided downstream generation and the step check rarely rejects valid prefixes.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Beam Search</strong>: The method first appeared in the HARPY speech recognition system, which advanced only a small set of the best-scoring partial paths at each step instead of the full search space [@lowerre1976]. It accepted a bounded risk of pruning the eventual best path in exchange for bounded time and memory, the same trade a runtime makes when it retains a fixed number of partial candidates.</p>\n<p>Rather than expanding every surviving candidate equally at every depth, tree search over intermediate reasoning steps [@yao2023tree] directs the next extension to whichever partial candidate looks most promising or most uncertain. <strong>Monte Carlo tree search</strong> [@coulom2006efficient; @kocsis2006bandit] tracks visit counts and average leaf scores along the edges of the search tree. Its PUCT selection rule from AlphaGo [@silver2016mastering] walks down from the root by picking at each partial state $\\PolicyObservedState$ the extension $\\PolicyAction$ that maximizes</p>\n<p>$$ \\TreeSearchMeanReturn(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction) + \\TreeSearchExplorationWeight\\,\\TreeSearchPriorDistribution(\\PolicyAction \\mid \\PolicyObservedState)\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{\\sum_{\\SiblingPolicyAction} \\TreeSearchVisitCount(\\PolicyObservedState,\\SiblingPolicyAction)}}{1 + \\TreeSearchVisitCount(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction)}, $$ {#eq-vol3-puct}</p>\n<p>where $\\TreeSearchMeanReturn(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction)$ is the mean leaf score seen on visits through extension $\\PolicyAction$, and $\\TreeSearchVisitCount(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction)$ is its visit count. The term $\\TreeSearchPriorDistribution(\\PolicyAction \\mid \\PolicyObservedState)$ is a prior distribution over extensions (such as the generator's probability for that step), and $\\TreeSearchExplorationWeight$ is a positive weight that balances high-scoring branches against plausible, less-visited ones. The exploration ratio grows with the square root of total visits across all sibling extensions at $\\PolicyObservedState$ while shrinking linearly with $1 + \\TreeSearchVisitCount(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction)$, so repeatedly visiting one branch diminishes its own bonus while raising the bonus of plausible siblings that have not yet been tried. The <strong>leaf value</strong> $\\TreeSearchLeafScore$ is the score assigned to a newly expanded partial candidate by a process scorer or step check, and $\\TreeSearchMeanReturn$ averages those leaf values across visits. When a new leaf returns value $\\TreeSearchLeafScore$, every edge along the path back to the root increments its visit count ($\\TreeSearchVisitCount \\leftarrow \\TreeSearchVisitCount + 1$) and updates its running mean ($\\TreeSearchMeanReturn \\leftarrow \\TreeSearchMeanReturn + (\\TreeSearchLeafScore - \\TreeSearchMeanReturn)/\\TreeSearchVisitCount$). If $\\TreeSearchLeafScore$ comes from a noisy learned scorer, $\\TreeSearchMeanReturn$ inherits the selection optimism of @eq-vol3-argmax-noise and pulls future visits toward branches whose early scores were inflated by noise. Tree search therefore helps only when its smarter allocation saves more work than leaf scoring and tree bookkeeping add.</p>\n<p>: <strong>From UCT to PUCT</strong>: The UCT rule treated each node's choice of extension as a multi-armed bandit, adding an exploration bonus that shrinks as an extension accumulates visits [@kocsis2006bandit]. PUCT weights that bonus by a predictor's prior over the arms [@rosin2011multi], which is what lets a language model's own step probabilities concentrate a small budget on plausible extensions. A miscalibrated prior steers visits away from correct but unlikely steps just as effectively.</p>\n<p>The three structures differ in where they prune (@fig-vol3-search-topologies). Best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$ waits until all candidates finish, beam search prunes at every step boundary, and tree search expands one favored leaf at a time while updating visit counts and mean values along its path. Those pruning points set each structure's concurrency, live state, and prefix sharing (@tbl-vol3-search-topologies).</p>\n<p>↪ Radix prefix caching, which lets later requests for a branch reuse the KV state of its shared ancestors, is developed in @sec-vol3-kv-cache-radix-lookup.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Structure</strong></th><th><strong>Where pruning happens</strong></th><th><strong>Concurrency</strong></th><th><strong>Search state</strong></th><th><strong>Prefix sharing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$</td><td>After complete candidates, at selection</td><td>All $\\CandidatePoolSize$ requests independent</td><td>$\\CandidatePoolSize$ candidates and their check results</td><td>The common prompt only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Beam</td><td>At each step boundary, by step score</td><td>Within a step; stages wait for scoring</td><td>Up to width times extensions before pruning, plus scores</td><td>Each retained prefix with its extensions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tree search</td><td>Adaptively, by value estimate and visits</td><td>Irregular; depends on which leaves are chosen</td><td>Tree nodes with visit counts and value estimates</td><td>Every node's ancestors along its path</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Search structures compared</strong>: Each structure moves the pruning decision to a different point and holds different state. Concurrency describes the dependency graph; actual overlap depends on service capacity. {#tbl-vol3-search-topologies tbl-colwidths=\"[13,22,21,23,21]\"}</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h3\">How step pruning bounds tree growth</h3>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ PRUNED TREE GROWTH (LEGO)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-tree-growth.\n# │ Goal: Show how a step check&#x27;s rejection rate bounds expected tree size.\n# │ Show: Unpruned leaf explosion; effective branching factor; generated nodes.\n# │ How: Independent rejection of each child; generated nodes are b times the\n# │      surviving parents at each depth; equal-sized steps.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\n\nclass PrunedTree:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    scenario = Scenarios.StepCheckedSearchTree\n    wide_b = scenario.wide_branching  # hypothetical alternatives per step, unpruned illustration\n    wide_depth = scenario.wide_depth  # hypothetical steps, unpruned illustration\n    b = scenario.branching  # hypothetical children proposed per retained node\n    p_prune = scenario.rejection_rate  # hypothetical probability a step check rejects a child\n    depth = scenario.depth  # hypothetical steps in the worked example\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    result = scenario.result\n    wide_leaves = result.wide_leaves\n    b_eff = result.effective_branching\n    critical_prune = result.critical_rejection\n    unpruned_generated = result.unpruned_nodes\n    pruned_generated = result.pruned_nodes\n    unpruned_leaves = result.unpruned_leaves\n    pruned_leaves = result.pruned_leaves\n    generated_saving = result.node_saving\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(wide_leaves &gt; 1_000_000, &quot;Unpruned illustration exceeds a million leaves&quot;)\n    check(b_eff &gt; 1, &quot;Worked example remains supercritical&quot;)\n    check(p_prune &lt; critical_prune, &quot;Rejection rate is below the subcritical threshold&quot;)\n    check(pruned_generated &lt; unpruned_generated, &quot;Pruning reduces generated nodes&quot;)\n    check(generated_saving &gt; p_prune, &quot;Node saving exceeds the per-child rejection rate&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ───────────────────────────────────────────\n    wide_b_str = fmt_int(wide_b)\n    wide_depth_str = fmt_int(wide_depth)\n    wide_leaves_str = fmt_int(wide_leaves)\n    b_str = fmt_int(b)\n    p_prune_str = fmt_percent(p_prune, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    depth_str = fmt_int(depth)\n    b_eff_str = fmt(b_eff, precision=1)\n    critical_prune_str = fmt_percent(critical_prune, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    unpruned_generated_str = fmt_int(unpruned_generated)\n    pruned_generated_str = fmt(pruned_generated, precision=1)\n    unpruned_leaves_str = fmt_int(unpruned_leaves)\n    pruned_leaves_str = fmt(pruned_leaves, precision=1)\n    generated_saving_str = fmt_percent(generated_saving, precision=1, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\nA search tree that expands every node into `[PrunedTree.wide_b_str]` branches across `[PrunedTree.wide_depth_str]` steps without pruning produces `[PrunedTree.wide_leaves_str]` leaves and quickly exhausts memory and token budgets. Step pruning bounds that growth. Suppose each retained node proposes $\\SearchBranchingFactor$ child extensions and a step check independently rejects each child with probability $\\ChildPruningProbability$. The expected number of surviving children per expanded node, the **effective branching factor**, is\n\n$$\n\\EffectiveBranchingFactor = \\SearchBranchingFactor\\,(1 - \\ChildPruningProbability).\n$$ {#eq-vol3-effective-branching}\n\nUnder @eq-vol3-effective-branching, the expected frontier width at depth $\\SearchDepthIndex$ is $\\EffectiveBranchingFactor^{\\,\\SearchDepthIndex}$. Because each surviving node at depth $\\SearchDepthIndex-1$ generates and checks $\\SearchBranchingFactor$ children, the expected total number of generated nodes through depth $\\SearchDepth$ is $\\sum_{\\SearchDepthIndex=1}^{\\SearchDepth} \\SearchBranchingFactor\\,\\EffectiveBranchingFactor^{\\,\\SearchDepthIndex-1}$. This is a Galton–Watson branching process. When $\\EffectiveBranchingFactor &lt; 1$, which requires a rejection rate above $1 - 1/\\SearchBranchingFactor$, the frontier shrinks geometrically with depth and dies out on its own. When $\\EffectiveBranchingFactor &gt; 1$, the expected frontier still grows exponentially, so hard ceilings on depth, tokens, or time must stop the search.\n\n: **Galton–Watson Process**: The model comes from a Victorian question about the extinction of family surnames, analyzed in 1875 [@watson1875probability]. That analysis wrongly concluded that every line dies out; the correct result is the threshold used here, under which extinction is certain when the mean number of surviving offspring is at most one (excluding the degenerate case of exactly one child every time) and avoidable when it exceeds one. For a search tree the surviving offspring are the children that pass the step check, so the rejection rate decides which side of the threshold the search lands on. \n\nSuppose each node proposes `[PrunedTree.b_str]` extensions, the step check rejects `[PrunedTree.p_prune_str]` of them, and the search runs to depth `[PrunedTree.depth_str]`. The effective branching factor is `[PrunedTree.b_eff_str]`. Because the `[PrunedTree.p_prune_str]` rejection rate sits below the critical rate of `[PrunedTree.critical_prune_str]` for $\\SearchBranchingFactor =$ `[PrunedTree.b_str]`, the tree remains supercritical ($\\EffectiveBranchingFactor &gt; 1$). Even so, an unpruned tree across three steps generates `[PrunedTree.unpruned_generated_str]` nodes and ends with `[PrunedTree.unpruned_leaves_str]` leaves. The pruned tree generates an expected `[PrunedTree.pruned_generated_str]` nodes and ends with `[PrunedTree.pruned_leaves_str]` leaves, cutting node generations by `[PrunedTree.generated_saving_str]`. The total saving beats the `[PrunedTree.p_prune_str]` single-step rejection rate because rejecting a parent also removes the entire subtree beneath it. With equal-sized steps, generated tokens drop by the same fraction, though each rejected node still pays for its own generation and step check.\n\n\n\nTwo practical limits qualify this calculation. First, a step check that falsely rejects valid steps kills good branches permanently, so aggressive pruning helps only when false rejections are rare. Second, when a step needs just one surviving child to move forward, proposing children one at a time takes $1/(1 - \\ChildPruningProbability)$ tries in expectation, whereas proposing a batch of $\\ChildBatchSize$ children in parallel takes $1/(1 - \\ChildPruningProbability^{\\,\\ChildBatchSize})$ rounds of $\\ChildBatchSize$ children each. Batching cuts wall-clock rounds at the price of generating more total children, the same latency-versus-work trade that notebook \\ref{nbk-03-best-of-n-pool-price} priced for best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$ sampling.\n\n### Isolating workspaces across search branches {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-mutable-sandbox-search}\n\nEvaluating those surviving branches in parallel requires workspace isolation, because if one search branch edits files in a shared workspace and a sibling branch then runs its tests against that modified working tree, the sibling&#x27;s test results no longer reflect its own proposed patch. Although a partial candidate is just data in memory while being decoded, testing it requires running code against a real filesystem and process state. Every branch that writes files or mutates state therefore needs its own isolated container, copy-on-write filesystem snapshot, or deterministic rollback before another branch runs, so that each observation belongs strictly to the candidate and workspace snapshot that produced it.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Copy-on-write task workspaces that keep each run&#x27;s file modifications private are developed in @sec-vol3-sandboxes-cow-provisioning.\n:::\n\nIsolating branches costs real system resources even when most branches are later pruned. Concurrent sandboxes consume host RAM, disk volumes, process slots, and network bandwidth, while their test logs accumulate in the runtime&#x27;s state. Even read-only diagnostic commands need sandboxed permissions so they cannot leak host credentials or environment variables into the search trace. Pruning a branch drops it from the search frontier, but backtracking in the search tree cannot unsend an outbound network call or undo a write to an external database. Sandbox boundaries block those external writes during search, and any external action the runtime does permit has to be budgeted as irreversible.\n\n### Accounting for the whole search {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-search-accounting}\n\nCollapsing tokens, test runtimes, and memory into a single composite &quot;search cost&quot; hides whichever physical limit the search will hit first, because a beam search that cuts generated output tokens nearly in half can still cost more in dollars once repeated prompt reads are counted. A search runtime therefore keeps four separate accounts: money spent, physical work by unit, elapsed wall-clock time, and peak live state. Money is the one common unit that adds up across generator tokens, scorer calls, and tool runs. Physical work, by contrast, comes in non-interchangeable kinds: prompt tokens, output tokens, scorer tokens, test executions, and sandbox startups each load different hardware resources, and adding model FLOPs to test seconds produces a meaningless number.\n\nWall-clock time depends on how the scheduler maps those operations onto hardware: sequential stages add along the critical path, concurrent branches overlap up to the available worker slots, and requests beyond those slots wait in waves. Peak live state is a fourth, separate limit, set by what must sit in memory and storage at the same instant (active KV caches, candidate trees, test logs, and live sandboxes) rather than the cumulative work over the whole run. Saving output tokens by pruning early often adds prompt re-reads, scoring barriers, or concurrent state, so keeping the four accounts separate shows which resource actually constrains the policy.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ BOUNDED SEARCH RESOURCE ACCOUNTS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-bounded-search,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          nbk-03-compute-allocation-and-latency-trade-offs.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal: Compare counted work, token charges, and scheduled time for two</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       hypothetical policies without assigning either a quality advantage.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show: Expansion counts, input/output/scorer tokens, prices, and time.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How: Registry-derived output allowance; explicit local workload, prices,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│      concurrency, and nonoverlapping generation/scoring/test stages.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Note: No prefix-reuse discount. Prefill and service overheads are omitted</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">│       from the timing subtotal, not from generator input-token charges.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import *</p>\n<p>class SearchBudget:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>scenario = Scenarios.FlatVsBeamSearch  # 8-bit Llama-3 70B decode floor, one H100 steps = scenario.segments  # hypothetical candidate length in equal-sized segments step_tokens = scenario.segment_tokens  # hypothetical newly generated tokens per extension prompt_tokens = scenario.prompt_tokens  # hypothetical common task prompt per request flat_candidates = scenario.flat_candidates  # hypothetical complete-candidate budget beam_width = scenario.beam_width  # hypothetical retained-prefix limit extensions = scenario.extensions  # hypothetical extensions per retained prefix generation_slots = scenario.generation_slots  # hypothetical concurrent streams, unchanged rate test_slots = scenario.test_slots  # hypothetical concurrent isolated test runs score_time = scenario.score_time  # fixed scenario allowance per FULL-prefix score test_time = scenario.test_time  # fixed scenario allowance per final test price_in = scenario.input_price  # hypothetical generator input rate price_out = scenario.output_price  # hypothetical output rate price_score = scenario.scorer_price  # hypothetical scorer input rate</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">The scorer reads each complete generated prefix, not only its suffix.</h1>\n<p>result = scenario.result token_time = result.token_time frontier_counts = result.frontier_counts retained = result.retained beam_prompt_tokens = result.beam_prompt_tokens scorer_tokens = result.scorer_tokens beam_waves = result.beam_waves beam_segments = result.beam_segments beam_output_tokens = result.beam_output_tokens flat_length = steps * step_tokens flat_output_tokens = result.flat_output_tokens flat_prompt_tokens = result.flat_prompt_tokens flat_waves = result.flat_waves flat_test_waves = result.flat_test_waves beam_test_waves = result.beam_test_waves beam_scoring_time = result.beam_scoring_time flat_elapsed = result.flat_elapsed beam_elapsed = result.beam_elapsed flat_token_cost = result.flat_token_cost beam_token_cost = result.beam_token_cost output_saving = result.output_saving</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">Break-even: the input-price fraction (e.g. under prefix caching) at which</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">the beam's output saving net of scoring pays for its repeated input.</h1>\n<p>out_saving_usd = result.output_saving_cost scorer_usd = result.scorer_cost input_premium_usd = result.input_premium_cost breakeven_input_share = result.breakeven_input_share</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD (Invariants) ───────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(frontier_counts == [3, 9, 12, 12], \"Root expansion precedes a full beam\") check(beam_segments == 36 and retained == 4, \"Count extensions before pruning\") check(flat_output_tokens == 8192 and beam_output_tokens == 4608, \"Count generated suffixes once\") check(scorer_tokens == 13440, \"Full-prefix scoring rereads ancestor tokens\") check(beam_prompt_tokens == 156288 and flat_prompt_tokens == 65536, \"Bill every supplied prompt without reuse discount\") check(beam_waves == 7 and flat_waves == 2, \"Apply generation concurrency to each stage\") check(abs(output_saving - 0.4375) &lt; 1e-12, \"Beam generates fewer output tokens in this scenario\") check(beam_token_cost &gt; flat_token_cost, \"Repeated inputs reverse the token-charge ranking\") check(0 &lt; breakeven_input_share &lt; 1, \"Beam breaks even only at a discounted input price\") check(22 <em> second &lt; flat_elapsed &lt; 23 </em> second, \"Flat timing subtotal remains in its stated range\") check(19 <em> second &lt; beam_elapsed &lt; 21 </em> second, \"Beam timing subtotal remains in its stated range\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT (Formatting) ──────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>steps_str = fmt_count(steps) step_tokens_str = fmt_count(step_tokens) prompt_tokens_str = fmt_count(prompt_tokens) flat_candidates_str = fmt_count(flat_candidates) beam_width_str = fmt_count(beam_width) extensions_str = fmt_count(extensions) generation_slots_str = fmt_count(generation_slots) test_slots_str = fmt_count(test_slots) token_time_ms_str = fmt_time(token_time, unit=ms, precision=3) score_time_ms_str = fmt_time(score_time, unit=ms, precision=0) test_time_s_str = fmt_time(test_time, unit=second, precision=1) price_in_str = fmt_usd((price_in <em> Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0) price_out_str = fmt_usd((price_out </em> Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0) price_score_str = fmt_usd((price_score * Mtoken).to(USD), precision=2) frontier_counts_str = MarkdownStr(\", \".join(str(n) for n in frontier_counts)) beam_segments_str = fmt_count(beam_segments) flat_length_str = fmt_count(flat_length) flat_output_tokens_str = fmt_count(flat_output_tokens) beam_output_tokens_str = fmt_count(beam_output_tokens) flat_prompt_tokens_str = fmt_count(flat_prompt_tokens) beam_prompt_tokens_str = fmt_count(beam_prompt_tokens) scorer_tokens_str = fmt_count(scorer_tokens) flat_waves_str = fmt_count(flat_waves) beam_waves_str = fmt_count(beam_waves) flat_test_waves_str = fmt_count(flat_test_waves) beam_test_waves_str = fmt_count(beam_test_waves) beam_scoring_time_s_str = fmt_time(beam_scoring_time, unit=second, precision=2) flat_elapsed_s_str = fmt_time(flat_elapsed, unit=second, precision=2) beam_elapsed_s_str = fmt_time(beam_elapsed, unit=second, precision=2) flat_token_cost_str = fmt_usd(flat_token_cost, precision=4) beam_token_cost_str = fmt_usd(beam_token_cost, precision=4) output_saving_pct_str = fmt_percent(output_saving, precision=2, style=\"prose\") input_premium_usd_str = fmt_usd(input_premium_usd, precision=3) out_saving_usd_str = fmt_usd(out_saving_usd, precision=3) breakeven_input_share_str = fmt_percent(breakeven_input_share, precision=1, style=\"prose\") ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A coding agent generates candidate patches in <code>[SearchBudget.steps_str]</code> segments of <code>[SearchBudget.step_tokens_str]</code> tokens. Compare <code>[SearchBudget.flat_candidates_str]</code> complete candidates with beam width <code>[SearchBudget.beam_width_str]</code> and <code>[SearchBudget.extensions_str]</code> extensions per retained prefix. How do early pruning and repeated scoring change the resource totals?</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong>: Every generator request supplies the same <code>[SearchBudget.prompt_tokens_str]</code>-token task prompt plus its retained generated prefix. The illustrative per-token generation time from @sec-vol3-foundation-model-per-call-work-estimate is <code>[SearchBudget.token_time_ms_str]</code>. Assume <code>[SearchBudget.generation_slots_str]</code> generation streams can maintain that rate concurrently. The scorer reads the entire generated prefix, excluding the common task prompt, and processes requests serially at a fixed <code>[SearchBudget.score_time_ms_str]</code> per request regardless of prefix length. The growth with prefix length that @eq-vol3-prm-rescoring describes therefore appears in the scorer's token bill rather than in this stipulated time. Each final test takes <code>[SearchBudget.test_time_s_str]</code>, with <code>[SearchBudget.test_slots_str]</code> isolated runs available concurrently. Generation, scoring, and final testing do not overlap in this schedule.</p>\n<p>Hypothetical prices per million tokens are <code>[SearchBudget.price_in_str]</code> for generator input, <code>[SearchBudget.price_out_str]</code> for output, and <code>[SearchBudget.price_score_str]</code> for scorer input. Input charges include every supplied token, without a prefix-reuse discount. The timing subtotal omits prefill, queueing, request transfer, workspace preparation, and cleanup. It is not a prediction of complete service latency, and the token-charge subtotal excludes execution and infrastructure charges.</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Multiplying <code>[SearchBudget.flat_candidates_str]</code> candidates by <code>[SearchBudget.flat_length_str]</code> output tokens per candidate gives <code>[SearchBudget.flat_output_tokens_str]</code> output tokens. Multiplying the same candidate count by the common prompt length gives <code>[SearchBudget.flat_prompt_tokens_str]</code> input tokens. Generation occupies <code>[SearchBudget.flat_waves_str]</code> waves of complete candidates, followed by <code>[SearchBudget.flat_test_waves_str]</code> test waves. Multiplying the generation waves by candidate length and the per-token allowance, then adding the test waves times test duration, gives <code>[SearchBudget.flat_elapsed_s_str]</code>. Multiplying each token count by its corresponding price gives a token-charge subtotal of <code>[SearchBudget.flat_token_cost_str]</code>.</p>\n<p>The beam expands frontiers of <code>[SearchBudget.frontier_counts_str]</code> extensions in successive stages, because the root expands <code>[SearchBudget.extensions_str]</code> ways in stage 1, those <code>[SearchBudget.extensions_str]</code> prefixes expand to 9 extensions in stage 2, and pruning retains at most the beam width (<code>[SearchBudget.beam_width_str]</code> prefixes) from stage 2 onward so each later stage expands <code>[SearchBudget.beam_width_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[SearchBudget.extensions_str]</code> = 12 extensions. Adding these counts gives <code>[SearchBudget.beam_segments_str]</code> newly generated segments, and multiplying by the segment length gives <code>[SearchBudget.beam_output_tokens_str]</code> output tokens. For each request, generator input is the common prompt plus the preceding generated segments, and summing across requests gives <code>[SearchBudget.beam_prompt_tokens_str]</code> input tokens. The scorer instead reads each complete generated prefix, including its new segment. Summing each stage's extension count times its prefix length gives <code>[SearchBudget.scorer_tokens_str]</code> scorer-input tokens. Because stages whose frontier exceeds the <code>[SearchBudget.generation_slots_str]</code> concurrent generation slots split into two waves, generation requires <code>[SearchBudget.beam_waves_str]</code> waves of one-segment requests, serial scoring adds <code>[SearchBudget.beam_scoring_time_s_str]</code>, and the final survivors fit in <code>[SearchBudget.beam_test_waves_str]</code> test wave. Applying the same duration and price arithmetic gives <code>[SearchBudget.beam_elapsed_s_str]</code> and <code>[SearchBudget.beam_token_cost_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: The beam generates <code>[SearchBudget.output_saving_pct_str]</code> fewer output tokens, worth <code>[SearchBudget.out_saving_usd_str]</code>, but resupplying the task prompt and retained prefixes to every extension costs <code>[SearchBudget.input_premium_usd_str]</code> more in input, so its token bill is the higher one. The knob is the price of repeated input. The beam's bill falls below flat sampling's only when input tokens cost less than <code>[SearchBudget.breakeven_input_share_str]</code> of list price, the regime a prefix cache creates. Its shorter generation waves already give it the lower timing subtotal under this schedule, before omitted prefill is added back. Quality does not appear in these totals; the choice between the two rests on the check results each produces.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h3\">Reserving budget before admitting work</h3>\n<p>Checking how many tokens a search has spent only after concurrent model calls return is too late to prevent a budget overrun, because eight in-flight calls can all finish and bill their full output allowance before the budget account updates. Before dispatching any candidate extension, the runtime must reserve the call's maximum allowed output tokens along with its scorer and sandbox allowances, keep settled spend plus open reservations below the search ceilings, and release unused capacity only after the call settles. A post-call usage receipt tells the runtime what to settle, but only a pre-dispatch reservation can bound concurrent spend in advance.</p>\n<p>↪ Charging every pending operation its worst-case ceiling until settlement is the bounded-liability admission developed in @sec-vol3-agent-harness-reservation-settlement.</p>\n<p>Search depth and wall-clock time also need separate limits. A depth cap limits the chain of sequential extensions or tool turns, while a deadline caps user-perceived latency. When the deadline expires, the runtime stops admitting new work, drops late responses from the expired phase, and sends cancellation signals to in-flight calls. As @sec-vol3-foundation-model-streaming-cancellation showed, however, sending a cancel request does not guarantee that the remote server stops immediately; a server that ignores cancellation can keep decoding and billing up to the call's full output ceiling. Until the remote service settles the request, the runtime must keep the full reservation charged against the budget.</p>\n<p>Running out of budget ends the search without lowering the acceptance bar. A finished candidate can still fail regression tests, and a high learned score cannot stand in for a required check pass. If no candidate meets the acceptance contract when a ceiling is reached, the runtime reports an incomplete result, abstains, or escalates to a human operator. That separation is invariant closure (principle \\ref{pri-invariant-closure}) applied to deliberation: mechanical limits sit below the model to stop spending, and acceptance evidence sits above the model to authorize returning an answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h3\">When to stop searching early</h3>\n<p>Hard ceilings stop a search when its allowance runs out, whereas early-stopping rules end the search inside the ceilings as soon as a candidate passes or the next round is no longer worth its cost. Many repair workloads need only the first candidate that meets their acceptance criteria, a stopping rule known as <strong>satisficing</strong>. Once a candidate passes the required checks and policy rules, the search stops and returns it. If the search stage checks only compilation, stopping at the first compiling patch hands testing and deployment authorization to the next stage of the pipeline.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Satisficing</strong>: Herbert Simon borrowed the word in 1956 to describe a chooser who stops at the first alternative meeting an aspiration level, because a decision maker with limited capacity cannot afford an exhaustive search for the best [@simon1956rational]. A runtime's acceptance criteria play the role of that aspiration level, which is why they must be fixed before search begins rather than lowered as the budget runs out.</p>\n<p>An explicit secondary objective can justify searching further after finding an acceptable candidate, such as looking for a smaller diff or a faster query plan within the remaining budget. A rising learned score by itself does not justify continuing if held-out benchmarks show that higher scores past the pass threshold rarely improve real outcomes. Continuing therefore requires an explicit estimate of what the next unit of work is worth.</p>\n<p><strong>Marginal stopping</strong> asks whether the expected acceptance gain from the next round is worth its added cost. During a live repair, the runtime does not know the exact probability that the next attempt will pass, so it estimates that gain from calibration curves measured on held-out tasks or from progress signals such as fewer failing tests. Because any single diagnostic is only a partial signal, those estimates hold only for workloads and test suites similar to the calibration set.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Sequential Stopping</strong>: Statistics made stopping part of an experiment's design with the sequential probability ratio test, which after each observation decides whether to accept, reject, or sample again [@wald1945sequential]. Such tests reach a verdict with fewer observations on average than a fixed-size test with the same error rates. The marginal rule borrows that structure, although its threshold is an economic choice rather than an error rate.</p>\n<p>For a nested sequence of round limits under the same workload and acceptance criterion, let $\\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount}$ estimate the probability of finding an acceptable result by round $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$. Let $\\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount}$ denote the expected cumulative expenditure of the policy that stops at its first acceptable result and otherwise runs up to that round limit. Before admitting round $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$, with no earlier acceptance, the marginal policy stops when</p>\n<p>$$ \\frac{\\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount} - \\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}}{\\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount} - \\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}} &lt; \\MarginalStoppingThreshold, $$ {#eq-vol3-marginal-stopping}</p>\n<p>where $\\MarginalStoppingThreshold$ is the minimum required gain in acceptance probability per unit of spend. Because round $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$ executes only when the first $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount - 1$ rounds fail to produce an acceptable result (an event with probability $1 - \\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}$), the denominator $\\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount} - \\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}$ equals the full cost of running round $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$ multiplied by $1 - \\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}$. Dividing both numerator and denominator by that same reach probability $1 - \\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}$ converts the numerator into the conditional probability of passing in round $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$ given earlier failure and cancels the reach probability from the denominator. The ratio in @eq-vol3-marginal-stopping therefore equals the conditional next-round pass probability divided by the full cost of running that round. Dividing the unconditional probability gain by the full round cost would mix two different denominators. In an adaptive search, these conditional estimates should be updated from the current search state rather than read blindly from an unconditional table.</p>\n<p>The numerator measures the gain in the estimated chance of a verified pass, not the number of new candidates or a rise in a heuristic score. The verification asymmetry (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-verification-asymmetry}) explains why, since generating more candidates and obtaining a verified solution are distinct events. A search whose calibrated chance of passing stays flat is burning budget without making progress, no matter how many tokens it emits. The threshold $\\MarginalStoppingThreshold$ is an economic choice set by the value of an automated fix relative to human escalation, net of checker error and delay; high failure costs never justify searching longer against an untrustworthy check.</p>\n<p>For satisficing workloads, the runtime applies these three stopping checks in a fixed order before admitting any new work (@fig-vol3-stopping-boundaries). First, hard ceilings on tokens, calls, depth, or wall-clock time stop new admissions unconditionally; if an acceptable candidate is already in hand it goes to the acceptance gate, and otherwise the runtime abstains or escalates. Second, within the ceilings, any candidate that meets the acceptance criteria stops the search and goes to the acceptance gate. Third, when the ceilings still have room and no candidate has passed yet, the marginal stopping rule (@eq-vol3-marginal-stopping) stops the search early if the next round's expected gain per dollar falls below $\\MarginalStoppingThreshold$. Reversing this order would either let an optimistic gain estimate breach a hard ceiling or let a diminishing-returns check discard a candidate that already passed. Whichever condition ends the search, the runtime settles all open reservations and separates verified passes from unverified attempts.</p>\n<p>The resource accounts and the stopping policy must count work even when it produces no accepted result.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Input budget</strong>: Using the notebook's prompt length and expansion counts, estimate the input tokens spent on common prompts alone and explain why they exceed flat sampling's input count.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Pending work</strong>: Which requests must remain reserved when the runtime reaches its deadline but remote cancellation is unconfirmed?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Stopping precedence</strong>: A search has nearly exhausted its token ceiling, holds a candidate that passes the sealed suite, and still estimates a marginal gain above $\\MarginalStoppingThreshold$. Which rule decides, and what does the runtime do next?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Acceptance evidence</strong>: What evidence would justify stopping with an accepted repair, and what should the runtime return if only the highest learned score is available?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Search structures, resource accounts, and stopping rules define how a single deliberation policy runs inside its bounds. Choosing among competing policies requires comparing their end-to-end pass rates, actual spend, and deadline completions on the same workload, charging every failed and timed-out attempt to the accepted results.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-20\" class=\"cs-h2\">Evaluating Deliberation Policies</h2>\n<p>Suppose an iterative search policy resolves more connection-pool failures than a single-call baseline. That gain might come from the search algorithm itself, or it might come from a larger token budget, a stronger base model, or silently dropping timed-out tasks from the denominator. Judging any policy that combines candidate allocation, selection, verification checks, and early stopping therefore requires recording three things: the exact resource ceilings and permissions granted to each policy, the tokens, checks, and wall-clock time actually spent, and the fate of every task that failed to produce an accepted result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-21\" class=\"cs-h3\">Controlled and system-level comparisons</h3>\n<p>Comparing a multi-round search on a newer model against a single call on an older model, or counting only the tasks that finished before the deadline, mixes model capability and survivor bias with the effect of the search policy. A controlled policy comparison isolates the search policy by holding the base model, serving setup, benchmark tasks, tool interfaces, and acceptance criteria fixed while varying only the deliberation rule under the same resource ceilings and deadline. Prompt tokens, output tokens, check runs, and concurrency slots should each be stated as separate ceilings rather than folded into one vague \"compute budget.\" Because a good policy stops early when a candidate passes, unspent budget reflects efficiency rather than an unfair test.</p>\n<p>↪ Complete agent policies are compared at matched budgets, with every initiated trial charged, by the method developed in @sec-vol3-evaluation-matched-budgets.</p>\n<p>A system-design comparison answers a broader engineering question: how two complete stacks trade cost, latency, and accuracy against one another. For instance, an engineer might compare one call to a large model against repeated calls to a smaller, faster model under the same dollar and latency caps. That comparison tells the team which stack to deploy, but it cannot separate the effect of search from the difference between the two models. Keeping controlled policy tests separate from stack-level comparisons prevents teams from crediting a model upgrade to their search algorithm.</p>\n<p>Evaluation logs must record every started task, including explicit failures, abstentions, and deadline timeouts. To avoid crediting a search for exploiting its own scorer (the Goodhart failure of @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-selection-pressure), final output quality must be graded by an independent check rather than the internal score that guided the search. Logs should also separate three failure modes: false acceptance (returning a broken candidate), abstention or escalation, and deadline timeout. When reporting checker error rates, state the denominator explicitly. The share of broken candidates that pass a check ($\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate$) differs from the share of passing candidates that are broken ($1-\\VerifierPrecision$). Because stochastic sampling and branching make those outcomes and error rates vary from run to run, benchmarks also need repeated trials across the task set rather than a single point number.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Two Error Denominators</strong>: The fraction of incorrect candidates that pass is the check's false positive rate, $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate$ in this chapter; the fraction of accepted outputs that are incorrect is the false discovery rate, $1-\\VerifierPrecision$. @Eq-vol3-verifier-precision connects them only through the valid-candidate rate, so a report that gives one without the base rate cannot be converted into the other.</p>\n<p>Total spend includes every resource used along the way: pruned branches, repeated prompt prefill across turns, verifier runs, and tokens decoded in flight after a cancellation signal. Dividing the total spend across the whole task cohort by the number of tasks that produced an accepted result gives the effective cost per accepted task, the cohort measure of $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$ (@eq-vol3-cost-per-accepted-task). Because a slow repair that misses the service deadline is unusable, the share of tasks that finish before the deadline belongs right beside $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$; a low average latency can hide a long tail where sequential revisions time out.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-22\" class=\"cs-h3\">Policies under a deadline</h3>\n<p>A deliberation policy that allows three feedback rounds on paper can run at most two rounds in practice when each round takes 30 seconds and the service deadline is 75 seconds. To see how concurrency limits and wall-clock deadlines constrain a policy in production, the three-policy comparison evaluates three policies under a scoped test suite rather than an omniscient oracle. All probabilities are stipulated parameters chosen to show how concurrency, stopping rules, and token budgets interact under one deadline.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THREE HYPOTHETICAL POLICIES UNDER A COMMON DEADLINE (LEGO)\n# │ Goal: Compare completed suite-pass opportunities and actual expenditure.\n# │ Show: Deadline-censored probability, expected tokens/checks, time, and bill.\n# │ How: Fixed round allowance; independent breadth; conditional feedback gain.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\n\nclass StrategyComparison:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD (Constants &amp; Parameters) ─────────────────────────────────\n    scenario = Scenarios.DeadlineBoundRepairPolicies  # 8-bit Llama-3 70B decode floor, one H100\n    prompt = scenario.prompt  # hypothetical fully serialized first request\n    output = scenario.output  # hypothetical generated tokens per candidate\n    diagnostic = scenario.diagnostic  # hypothetical failure-report tokens\n    breadth = scenario.breadth  # independent candidates, all generated and checked\n    max_rounds = scenario.max_rounds  # requested feedback cap, before deadline admission\n    input_ceiling = scenario.input_ceiling\n    output_ceiling = scenario.output_ceiling\n    check_ceiling = scenario.check_ceiling\n    concurrency = scenario.concurrency  # stipulated generator and check capacity\n    deadline = scenario.deadline\n    check_time = scenario.check_time  # distinct from earlier notebook scenarios\n    initial_pass = scenario.initial_pass  # scoped suite-pass probability for a fixed task\n    revision_pass = scenario.revision_pass  # conditional probability after a failed first attempt\n    price_in = scenario.input_price  # hypothetical per-million-input-token price\n    price_out = scenario.output_price  # hypothetical per-million-output-token price\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE (Compute) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n    result = scenario.result\n    token_time = result.token_time\n    round_time = result.round_time\n    rounds = result.rounds\n    revision_input = result.revision_input\n    a_pass, b_pass, c_pass = result.a_pass, result.b_pass, result.c_pass\n    c_rounds = result.c_rounds\n    a_input, b_input, c_input = result.a_input, result.b_input, result.c_input\n    a_output, b_output, c_output = result.a_output, result.b_output, result.c_output\n    a_cost, b_cost, c_cost = result.a_cost, result.b_cost, result.c_cost\n    c_time = result.c_time\n    max_c_time = result.max_c_time\n    requested_time = result.requested_time\n    a_bill_per_pass = result.a_cost_per_pass  # model bill per suite-passing task\n    b_bill_per_pass = result.b_cost_per_pass\n    c_bill_per_pass = result.c_cost_per_pass\n    b_over_a = result.b_over_a\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD (Validate Assumptions &amp; Bounds) ─────────────────────────\n    check(rounds == 2, &quot;this notebook&#x27;s conditional revision model has two rounds&quot;)\n    check(0 &lt;= initial_pass &lt;= 1 and 0 &lt;= revision_pass &lt;= 1, &quot;pass probabilities in [0, 1]&quot;)\n    check(all(0 &lt;= probability &lt;= 1 for probability in (a_pass, b_pass, c_pass)), &quot;probabilities in [0, 1]&quot;)\n    check(max_c_time &lt;= deadline &lt; requested_time, &quot;deadline bounds&quot;)\n    check(prompt &gt; 0 and output &gt; 0 and diagnostic &gt;= 0, &quot;token counts positive&quot;)\n    check(round_time &gt; 0 * second and check_time &gt;= 0 * second, &quot;times non-negative&quot;)\n    check(breadth &lt;= min(concurrency, check_ceiling), &quot;breadth within concurrency and check ceiling&quot;)\n    check(rounds &lt;= check_ceiling, &quot;rounds within check ceiling&quot;)\n    check(max(b_input, prompt + revision_input) &lt;= input_ceiling, &quot;input within ceiling&quot;)\n    check(max(b_output, rounds * output) &lt;= output_ceiling, &quot;output within ceiling&quot;)\n    check(a_bill_per_pass &lt; c_bill_per_pass &lt; b_bill_per_pass, &quot;cost ordering&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Typed Prose Strings) ──────────────────────────────────\n    prompt_str = fmt_int(prompt)\n    output_str = fmt_int(output)\n    diagnostic_str = fmt_int(diagnostic)\n    breadth_str = fmt_int(breadth)\n    rounds_str = fmt_int(rounds)\n    max_rounds_str = fmt_int(max_rounds)\n    input_ceiling_str = fmt_int(input_ceiling)\n    output_ceiling_str = fmt_int(output_ceiling)\n    check_ceiling_str = fmt_int(check_ceiling)\n    concurrency_str = fmt_int(concurrency)\n    deadline_str = fmt_time(deadline, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=0)\n    check_time_str = fmt_time(check_time, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=0)\n    token_time_str = fmt_time(token_time, unit=&quot;ms&quot;, precision=3)\n    round_time_str = fmt_time(round_time, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=2)\n    requested_time_str = fmt_time(requested_time, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=2)\n    max_c_time_str = fmt_time(max_c_time, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=2)\n    c_time_str = fmt_time(c_time, unit=&quot;s&quot;, precision=2)\n    initial_pass_str = fmt_percent(initial_pass, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    revision_pass_str = fmt_percent(revision_pass, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    b_pass_str = fmt_percent(b_pass, precision=2, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    c_pass_str = fmt_percent(c_pass, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    c_no_pass_str = fmt_percent(1 - c_pass, precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n    c_rounds_str = fmt_ratio(c_rounds, precision=1)\n    price_in_str = fmt_usd((price_in * Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0)\n    price_out_str = fmt_usd((price_out * Mtoken).to(USD), precision=0)\n    a_input_str, b_input_str, c_input_str = fmt_int(a_input), fmt_int(b_input), fmt_ratio(c_input, precision=1, commas=True)\n    a_output_str, b_output_str, c_output_str = fmt_int(a_output), fmt_int(b_output), fmt_int(c_output)\n    a_cost_str, b_cost_str, c_cost_str = fmt_usd(a_cost, precision=5), fmt_usd(b_cost, precision=5), fmt_usd(c_cost, precision=5)\n    a_per_pass_str = fmt_int(result.a_output_per_pass)\n    b_per_pass_str = fmt_int(result.b_output_per_pass)\n    c_per_pass_str = fmt_int(result.c_output_per_pass)\n    a_bill_per_pass_str = fmt_usd(a_bill_per_pass, precision=4)\n    b_bill_per_pass_str = fmt_usd(b_bill_per_pass, precision=4)\n    c_bill_per_pass_str = fmt_usd(c_bill_per_pass, precision=4)\n    b_over_a_mult_str = fmt_multiple(b_over_a, precision=1)\n]```\n\n::: {#nbk-03-strategy-comparison .callout-notebook title=&quot;Three policies, one deadline&quot;}\n\n**Scenario**: Consider an automated software repair workload checked by a sealed regression suite. An initial candidate passes the suite with hypothetical probability $\\AttemptPassProbability$ = `[StrategyComparison.initial_pass_str]`, and repeated attempts on the same task draw candidates independently. Passing the suite is the acceptance rule for this stage, though passing tests does not guarantee that the patch is defect-free. Each policy runs under the same permissions and resource ceilings: at most `[StrategyComparison.input_ceiling_str]` input tokens, `[StrategyComparison.output_ceiling_str]` generated tokens, and `[StrategyComparison.check_ceiling_str]` test runs, with up to `[StrategyComparison.concurrency_str]` parallel workers and a hard `[StrategyComparison.deadline_str]` deadline.\n\nEach initial request sends `[StrategyComparison.prompt_str]` prompt tokens, each candidate generates `[StrategyComparison.output_str]` tokens, and running the test suite takes `[StrategyComparison.check_time_str]`. Using the illustrative `[StrategyComparison.token_time_str]` per-token decode time from @sec-vol3-foundation-model-per-call-work-estimate, assume that concurrent streams keep the same per-token rate and that tests run on dedicated workers without contention (omitting prefill, queueing, network transfer, and filtering overhead). Token prices are `[StrategyComparison.price_in_str]` per million prompt tokens and `[StrategyComparison.price_out_str]` per million output tokens, with no prefix-cache discount.\n\n**Option A (single candidate)**: Generate and test one candidate. It passes the suite with probability `[StrategyComparison.initial_pass_str]`, spending `[StrategyComparison.a_input_str]` prompt tokens, `[StrategyComparison.a_output_str]` generated tokens, and one test run. The attempt finishes in `[StrategyComparison.round_time_str]` at a model bill of `[StrategyComparison.a_cost_str]`, and if the test fails the runtime abstains.\n\n**Option B (parallel breadth)**: Dispatch all `[StrategyComparison.breadth_str]` candidates at once without early stopping, test each as it finishes, and return the first passing patch in index order. At least one of the $\\CandidatePoolSize$ = `[StrategyComparison.breadth_str]` candidates passes with probability pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize = 1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^{\\CandidatePoolSize}$ = `[StrategyComparison.b_pass_str]`, which is also the chance of returning an accepted repair. Because all eight streams and tests fit within the concurrency limit, wall-clock time stays `[StrategyComparison.round_time_str]`, while the fan-out spends `[StrategyComparison.b_input_str]` prompt tokens, `[StrategyComparison.b_output_str]` generated tokens, and `[StrategyComparison.breadth_str]` test runs for a model bill of `[StrategyComparison.b_cost_str]`.\n\n**Option C (sequential feedback)**: Generate one candidate and stop right away if it passes. If it fails, append the failed patch and a `[StrategyComparison.diagnostic_str]`-token failure trace to the prompt and request a revision, which passes with conditional probability $\\RevisionPassProbability$ = `[StrategyComparison.revision_pass_str]`. Although the policy nominally allows `[StrategyComparison.max_rounds_str]` rounds, three sequential rounds would take `[StrategyComparison.requested_time_str]` and miss the `[StrategyComparison.deadline_str]` deadline. Only `[StrategyComparison.rounds_str]` complete rounds fit inside `[StrategyComparison.deadline_str]`, so the deadline caps the policy at one revision and forces an abstention if the second attempt also fails.\n\n**Math**: With the third round cut off by the deadline, sequential feedback passes with probability $\\AttemptPassProbability+(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)\\,\\RevisionPassProbability$ = `[StrategyComparison.c_pass_str]` and runs an expected $1+(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)$ = `[StrategyComparison.c_rounds_str]` rounds. Expected spend is `[StrategyComparison.c_input_str]` prompt tokens, `[StrategyComparison.c_output_str]` generated tokens, and `[StrategyComparison.c_rounds_str]` test runs, for an expected model bill of `[StrategyComparison.c_cost_str]` and an expected latency of `[StrategyComparison.c_time_str]` (at most `[StrategyComparison.max_c_time_str]`). The remaining `[StrategyComparison.c_no_pass_str]` of tasks end without an accepted patch and must be counted as failed attempts. Across all started tasks, expected generated tokens per accepted repair are about `[StrategyComparison.a_per_pass_str]` for Option A, `[StrategyComparison.b_per_pass_str]` for Option B, and `[StrategyComparison.c_per_pass_str]` for Option C.\n\n**Systems insight**: Per accepted repair, the model bill is `[StrategyComparison.a_bill_per_pass_str]` for Option A, `[StrategyComparison.c_bill_per_pass_str]` for Option C, and `[StrategyComparison.b_bill_per_pass_str]` for Option B (`[StrategyComparison.b_over_a_mult_str]` the single-candidate baseline). Sequential feedback lifts the pass rate from `[StrategyComparison.initial_pass_str]` to `[StrategyComparison.c_pass_str]` at a small cost premium per accepted task, whereas parallel breadth reaches `[StrategyComparison.b_pass_str]` at a much steeper bill per accepted repair.\n\nThe deadline decides which policy can run. When the service has room for only one `[StrategyComparison.round_time_str]` round, parallel breadth is the only option here that improves on the `[StrategyComparison.initial_pass_str]` single-call pass rate. When `[StrategyComparison.deadline_str]` allows two rounds, sequential feedback raises the pass rate at a much smaller token bill than breadth, while a third round at `[StrategyComparison.requested_time_str]` would never finish in time. In production, test-runner cost and false acceptance rates belong alongside the token bill so that passing the suite tracks real task fixes.\n\n:::\n\n### Choosing an operating point {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-operating-point}\n\nNo single policy in the three-way repair comparison wins on every axis: the single call is cheapest, parallel breadth finishes in one round at the highest pass rate, and two-round feedback reaches an intermediate pass rate at a much lower token bill than breadth. Each of the three sits on the Pareto frontier. A policy is strictly dominated only when another feasible policy matches or beats it on every metric (latency, token bill, checking load, and pass rate) and strictly beats it on at least one. Among non-dominated policies, the service&#x27;s deadline, budget cap, and error tolerance pick the operating point.\n\nPicking that operating point requires testing under the same limits the service will enforce in production. Analytical formulas cannot plug an ad-hoc &quot;effective pool size&quot; into pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize$ when worker queues, prefix caching, and check runtimes reshape the actual cost and latency. Likewise, the gain from a feedback round depends on how informative the test failure trace actually is, and hard deadlines cut off multi-round searches before their planned final round. Benchmarks must therefore enforce the real worker timeouts, early-exit rules, and admission ceilings rather than extrapolating uncensored curves into a deadline-bound service.\n\n## Fallacies and Pitfalls {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-fallacies}\n\nThe most common mistakes in test-time compute come from treating extra work as proof of progress. Extra tokens, wider pools, and longer searches pay off only when the pool holds diverse hypotheses, the check separates valid repairs from plausible mistakes, and the budget account counts every discarded branch. When any of those pieces is missing, spending more compute only burns budget faster or selects more reliably for checker blind spots.\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Fallacy**: *More generated tokens necessarily produce a better answer.*\n\nExtended reasoning can help a model trace constraints already present in the prompt, but it can just as easily elaborate and defend a wrong initial guess. When the open question concerns runtime behavior, generating tokens cannot supply the missing observation. In @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-insufficient-response, generating a `[ProbeAllocation.answer_tokens_str]`-token explanation takes `[ProbeAllocation.answer_time_str]`, `[ProbeAllocation.time_mult_str]` the `[ProbeAllocation.probe_time_str]` test run that directly separates the two hypotheses. Token count measures spend, not progress; extra reasoning tokens earn their keep only when benchmarks show they raise the accepted pass rate enough to justify their latency and cost.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Pitfall**: *Treating independent samples as independent capabilities.*\n\nSamples drawn independently for a given prompt still share the same model weights and blind spots, so plugging a workload&#x27;s average pass rate into pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ overstates what breadth can deliver (@sec-vol3-test-time-compute-pass-at-k). Suppose half the tasks in a workload pass at `[BestOfN.p_easy_str]` per candidate and the other half never pass ($\\AttemptPassProbability = 0$). The average single-sample pass rate across the workload is `[BestOfN.p_str]`, the same rate that notebook \\ref{nbk-03-best-of-n-pool-price} used for a single task. Plugging that average into $1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^{\\CandidatePoolSize}$ predicts `[BestOfN.naive_mix_str]` success for a pool of `[BestOfN.n_max_str]`, whereas the true success rate across the workload is only `[BestOfN.true_mix_str]` because sampling never solves the half where $\\AttemptPassProbability = 0$. Always compute pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ per task first and average afterward.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Fallacy**: *A check with a low false acceptance rate makes almost every pass trustworthy.*\n\nHow trustworthy a passing verdict is depends as much on the generator&#x27;s base rate of valid candidates as on the check&#x27;s error rates (@eq-vol3-verifier-precision). When valid candidates are scarce, even a small false acceptance rate lets broken candidates fill a large share of the passing pool. With a `[CheckYield.eps_fa_str]` false acceptance rate, a `[CheckYield.eps_fr_str]` false rejection rate, and a `[CheckYield.p_str]` base rate of valid candidates, only `[CheckYield.precision_str]` of passing candidates are valid, and a first-passing selector returns a broken repair on `[CheckYield.wrong_str]` of task attempts (notebook \\ref{nbk-03-compute-overhead-and-yield-under}). On hard tasks, raising the generator&#x27;s base rate matters just as much for precision as tightening the check.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Pitfall**: *Charging only for the final answer.*\n\nPruned branches, repeated prompt prefill, test runs, and scorer calls all consume resources, and in-flight requests canceled at a deadline often keep billing until the remote server settles them. Pricing a deliberation policy by the tokens in its final returned answer hides most of its cost. In notebook \\ref{nbk-03-strategy-comparison}, parallel breadth returns a single `[StrategyComparison.output_str]`-token patch, but it bills `[StrategyComparison.b_output_str]` output tokens per task and spends `[StrategyComparison.b_per_pass_str]` output tokens per passing task. Likewise, the beam search in notebook \\ref{nbk-03-compute-allocation-and-latency-trade-offs} reads `[SearchBudget.beam_prompt_tokens_str]` input tokens to deliver `[SearchBudget.beam_width_str]` final survivors. Production cost accounts must charge every discarded branch and failed attempt to the accepted tasks.\n:::\n\n::: {.fallacy-pitfall}\n**Fallacy**: *The highest-scoring candidate has completed the task.*\n\nA scorer orders candidates on whatever proxy it was built to measure, and picking the maximum score concentrates positive scoring errors into the winner. With equal true utilities and independent Gaussian noise, the winner among `[ArgmaxSelection.n_large_str]` candidates has an expected scoring error of about `[ArgmaxSelection.shift_large_str]` standard deviations (@eq-vol3-argmax-noise), and a checker loophole present in only `[ArgmaxSelection.gap_rate_str]` of single candidates shows up in `[ArgmaxSelection.gap_present_str]` of `[ArgmaxSelection.n_large_str]`-candidate pools. A high relative score is neither a proof nor an authorization for properties the verifier did not test. When the budget runs out without a candidate passing the acceptance check, the top-scoring candidate is still an unverified failure.\n:::\n\n## Summary {#sec-vol3-ch3-summary}\n\nTest-time compute turns the cost of a decision into an explicit runtime trade-off. While model weights fix what a single forward pass can do, the runtime chooses how much extra work to spend, whether to spend it on depth, breadth, or feedback, and what evidence justifies stopping. Extra deliberation lowers cost per accepted task only when three links all hold. Generation surfaces a valid candidate that a single call missed, the selector picks that candidate out of the pool, and the verifier checks the property the task actually requires. If any link fails, attempt cost rises while the acceptance rate $\\AcceptanceProbability$ stalls, driving up $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$. A deliberation policy should therefore be judged like any other systems component: by the accepted tasks it delivers per dollar and per second within the service deadline. @Tbl-vol3-test-time-compute-key-equations collects the equations derived in this chapter for pricing allocations, measuring pool availability and check precision, bounding search trees, and stopping early.\n\n| **Equation**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | **What it bounds or decides**                                                                                                                | **Where derived**                     |\n|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------|\n| $\\DepthCriticalPathLatency \\approx \\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength) + \\ExtendedDepthLength\\,\\PerTokenDecodeTime$, $\\; \\BreadthCriticalPathLatency \\approx \\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength) + \\max_{n} \\DecodeInterval(\\OutputLength_n)$                                                                                                                              | Critical path of extending one candidate vs. sampling $\\CandidatePoolSize$ concurrently, given capacity for all $\\CandidatePoolSize$ streams | @eq-vol3-allocation-critical-path     |\n| $\\FeedbackCriticalPathLatency \\approx \\sum_{t=1}^{\\TurnHorizon}\\bigl(\\PrefillTime(\\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)}) + \\DecodeInterval(\\OutputLength_t) + {\\FeedbackCheckDuration}_{\\TurnIndex}\\bigr)$, $\\; \\SequenceLength^{(\\TurnIndex)} = \\BasePromptLength + \\sum_{j&lt;t}(\\OutputLength_j + \\lvert \\TrajectoryObservation_j\\rvert)$                             | Latency and input growth of observation-conditioned revision under an append-only context                                                    | @eq-vol3-allocation-critical-path     |\n| $\\operatorname{pass@}\\IndependentAttemptCount = 1-(1-\\AttemptPassProbability)^\\IndependentAttemptCount$, $\\; \\PassAtKEstimate = 1-\\binom{\\GeneratedSampleCount-\\PassingSampleCount}{\\IndependentAttemptCount}/\\binom{\\GeneratedSampleCount}{\\IndependentAttemptCount}$                                                                                         | Candidate availability for one task, and the ceiling on any selector that returns an unchanged pool member                                   | @sec-vol3-test-time-compute-pass-at-k |\n| $\\CostPerAcceptedTask = \\mathbb{E}[\\AttemptCost]/\\AcceptanceProbability$                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | How acceptance probability and expected attempt cost jointly determine cost per accepted task                                                | @eq-vol3-cost-per-accepted-task       |\n| $\\VerifierPrecision = \\ValidCandidateRate(1-\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate) / [\\ValidCandidateRate(1-\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate) + (1-\\ValidCandidateRate)\\,\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate]$                                                                                                                                                                       | Share of passing candidates that are valid; unchanged by pool size at fixed rates                                                            | @eq-vol3-verifier-precision           |\n| $\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ScoredStepCount}(\\SequenceLength + \\ScoredStepIndex\\,\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength) = \\ScoredStepCount \\SequenceLength + \\tfrac{\\ScoredStepCount(\\ScoredStepCount+1)}{2}\\,\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength$                                                                                                                                 | Scorer input for step-level checks that rescore each growing prefix without reuse                                                            | @eq-vol3-prm-rescoring                |\n| $\\mathbb{E}\\bigl[\\max_{i \\le \\CandidatePoolSize} \\CandidateScoringError_i\\bigr] \\approx \\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation\\sqrt{2\\ln \\CandidatePoolSize}$, $\\; 1-(1-\\ExploitCandidateFraction)^\\CandidatePoolSize$                                                                                                                                                 | Maximum-noise bound on the selected scoring error, and the chance a pool holds a check exploit                                               | @eq-vol3-argmax-noise                 |\n| $\\TreeSearchMeanReturn(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction) + \\TreeSearchExplorationWeight\\,\\TreeSearchPriorDistribution(\\PolicyAction \\mid \\PolicyObservedState)\\,\\sqrt{\\textstyle\\sum_{\\SiblingPolicyAction} \\TreeSearchVisitCount(\\PolicyObservedState,\\SiblingPolicyAction)}\\,/\\,\\bigl(1 + \\TreeSearchVisitCount(\\PolicyObservedState,\\PolicyAction)\\bigr)$ | Which extension of a partial candidate receives the next unit of tree search                                                                 | @eq-vol3-puct                         |\n| $\\EffectiveBranchingFactor = \\SearchBranchingFactor\\,(1 - \\ChildPruningProbability)$, $\\; \\sum_{\\SearchDepthIndex=1}^{\\SearchDepth} \\SearchBranchingFactor\\,\\EffectiveBranchingFactor^{\\,\\SearchDepthIndex-1}$                                                                                                                                                 | Whether pruning keeps a search tree finite, and the expected nodes it generates through depth $\\SearchDepth$                                 | @eq-vol3-effective-branching          |\n| $(\\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount} - \\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1})/(\\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount} - \\CumulativeWorkCost_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount-1}) &lt; \\MarginalStoppingThreshold$                                                                                                             | Early stop after the hard-limit and satisficing checks, using incremental expected stopped-policy cost                                       | @eq-vol3-marginal-stopping            |\n\n: **Key equations**: Each relation holds under the assumptions stated where it is derived, such as independent candidates for a fixed task, fixed checker error rates, and an unchanged per-token rate across concurrent streams. {#tbl-vol3-test-time-compute-key-equations tbl-colwidths=&quot;[46,36,18]&quot;}\n\n::: {.callout-takeaways title=&quot;More work needs a bounded policy&quot;}\n\n* **Observe when the question is empirical**: Generating more tokens re-examines what is already in the prompt, whereas running an external check brings in new runtime facts. In the connection-pool repair, generating one explanatory trace took `[ProbeAllocation.time_mult_str]` as long as the targeted probe that separated the two hypotheses.\n* **A pool is only as good as its selector**: Pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ caps what an unchanged-member selector can return, not what it actually returns. Averaging pass rates across a mixed workload naively predicts `[BestOfN.naive_mix_str]` success across `[BestOfN.n_max_str]` candidates when the true pool delivers only `[BestOfN.true_mix_str]`, because extra samples cannot solve tasks where the model has zero chance of success.\n* **A pass inherits the base rate**: When valid candidates make up `[CheckYield.p_str]` of the generator&#x27;s output, a verifier with a `[CheckYield.eps_fa_str]` false acceptance rate leaves only `[CheckYield.precision_str]` of passing candidates valid. Under the fixed IID first-passing rule, returned candidates have that same precision regardless of pool size. With equal true utilities and independent Gaussian noise, picking the top score among `[ArgmaxSelection.n_large_str]` candidates inflates the winner&#x27;s score by about `[ArgmaxSelection.shift_large_str]` standard deviations of noise.\n* **Revise along recorded dependencies**: When an observation refutes an assumption, regenerate only the artifacts that depend on it rather than rebuilding the whole candidate plan. Missing dependency edges let stale conclusions survive silently, and plan revision never undoes side effects already committed to external systems.\n* **Budgets end searches, not uncertainty**: Every pruned branch, verifier call, and failed attempt belongs in the cost per accepted task $\\CostPerAcceptedTask$, and service deadlines bound how many sequential rounds can run. When a budget or deadline runs out without an accepted candidate, the runtime must abstain or escalate rather than returning the top-scoring unverified candidate.\n\n:::\n\nThe verification asymmetry (principle \\ref{pri-vol3-verification-asymmetry}) gives a practical order for diagnosing any deliberation policy whose cost per accepted task is too high. First check pool availability per task via pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize$. If passing candidates rarely enter the pool, switch to a stronger model or escalate rather than sampling wider. If the pass rate for a task is zero under the current prompt and model, more samples will not help. Where availability is healthy, check $\\VerifierPrecision$ at the measured base rate, because a low precision means a better generator or a stricter check buys more than a larger pool. Only when both availability and precision are sound does the question become how many rounds of search to buy, which the marginal stopping rule answers in conditional acceptance gain per dollar. At every step, the runtime, not the model, decides whether to continue, abstain, or escalate.\n\n## Historical Perspective and Further Reading {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-history}\n\nSpending compute at decision time goes back to the earliest game-playing programs. Claude Shannon&#x27;s 1950 design for a chess program separated generating candidate moves from scoring the resulting board positions with a heuristic evaluation function [@shannon1950programming]. Because game trees grow exponentially with depth, searching every branch to the end was impossible, and heuristic search, which generates candidates and prunes weak branches with domain evaluations, became a central theme of artificial intelligence [@newell1976computer]. Beam search entered through speech recognition in HARPY [@lowerre1976], while the two stopping rules used in this chapter came from statistics and economics: Abraham Wald&#x27;s sequential probability ratio test [@wald1945sequential] and Herbert Simon&#x27;s satisficing [@simon1956rational].\n\nDependency-directed revision has an equally long history in systems and planning. Early robotics planners such as STRIPS and PLANEX checked plan preconditions against sensor readings and replanned when an observation contradicted an assumed state [@fikes1972learning]. Truth maintenance systems [@doyle1979truth] and `make` [@feldman1979make] turned that idea into the explicit dependency graphs that revisable candidate plans use today. As search spaces grew wider, Monte Carlo tree search [@coulom2006efficient; @kocsis2006bandit] introduced bandit-based tree expansion, and AlphaGo combined PUCT with neural policy and value networks to steer search through huge branching factors [@silver2016mastering].\n\nLanguage models brought these search mechanisms together over text and code. In program synthesis, pass@$\\IndependentAttemptCount$ measured how much parallel sampling improves functional test passage [@kulal2019spoc; @chen2021evaluating], and @cobbe2021training showed that training a separate verifier to rank sampled math solutions could beat scaling generator parameters alone. Chain-of-thought prompting extended depth within a single call [@wei2022cot], and self-consistency combined breadth with majority voting [@wang2022self]. Process supervision moved learned scoring from final answers to intermediate steps [@uesato2022solving; @lightman2023let], which enabled Tree of Thoughts [@yao2023tree] to run beam and tree search over multi-step reasoning.\n\nMore recent work treats test-time compute as an explicit budget to allocate across axes. @snell2024scaling showed that the best mix of sequential revision, parallel sampling, and verifier-guided search depends on task difficulty. Easy tasks favor revision, moderate tasks favor hybrid search, and tasks where the base model has near-zero success need a stronger model rather than more samples. Reinforcement learning on verifiable tasks then trained models to run long internal scratchpads that test ideas and backtrack inside a single generation [@deepseekai2025deepseekr1]. Finally, pushing search hard against learned reward models exposed Goodhart&#x27;s law [@goodhart1984] in the form of reward-model overoptimization [@gao2023scaling], where the winner&#x27;s proxy score keeps climbing while held-out accuracy peaks and falls.\n\n- **Search as problem solving** [@newell1976computer]. Introduces heuristic search as generating and pruning hypotheses under resource limits, the framework that test-time compute policies build on.\n- **Monte Carlo tree search** [@browne2012survey]. Surveys tree-search selection rules, rollouts, and backup operators adapted for multi-step language-model search.\n- **Trained verifiers** [@cobbe2021training]. Shows that sampling candidates and ranking them with a trained verifier can beat scaling generator parameters alone.\n- **Process supervision** [@lightman2023let]. Compares outcome-level and step-level supervision, detailing the labeling cost and pruning benefits of process reward models.\n- **Reward-model overoptimization** [@gao2023scaling]. Measures how proxy reward scores diverge from held-out accuracy as best-of-$\\CandidatePoolSize$ pool size grows.\n- **Compute-optimal test-time allocation** [@snell2024scaling]. Analyzes how the frontier across parallel sampling, revision, and tree search shifts with task difficulty under a fixed compute budget.\n\n## Exercises {#sec-vol3-test-time-compute-exercises}\n\nUnless a problem states otherwise, use the hypothetical givens in @tbl-vol3-test-time-compute-exercise-givens. They are problem inputs chosen for round arithmetic, not measurements of any model or service. Omit prefill, queueing, and transport time, and apply no prefix-cache discount unless a problem introduces one.\n\n| **Quantity**                                 | **Symbol**            | **Given**                 |\n|:---------------------------------------------|:----------------------|:--------------------------|\n| Generation time per output token, per stream | $\\PerTokenDecodeTime$ | 20 ms                     |\n| Generator input price                        |                       | \\$3 per million tokens    |\n| Generator output price                       |                       | \\$15 per million tokens   |\n| Learned-scorer input price                   |                       | \\$0.50 per million tokens |\n\n: **Givens for the exercises**: Hypothetical rates shared by the problems below. Concurrent streams keep the per-token rate up to the concurrency a problem states. {#tbl-vol3-test-time-compute-exercise-givens tbl-colwidths=&quot;[56,18,26]&quot;}\n\n**1. Three allocations, one repository (Basic).** A repair request carries an $8{,}000$-token prompt and a ceiling of $6{,}000$ generated tokens. Depth spends the whole ceiling on one candidate. Breadth generates six $1{,}000$-token candidates, but the service admits only three concurrent streams. Feedback runs $\\TurnHorizon = 3$ rounds of $1{,}000$-token candidates; each round ends with a 6 s check that returns a 500-token report, and later requests append every earlier candidate and report.\n\n(a) Using @eq-vol3-allocation-critical-path, compute each allocation&#x27;s critical path, input tokens, output tokens, and model-token bill.\n(b) The repository context grows to $64{,}000$ tokens. Recompute the three bills and the breadth premium $(\\CandidatePoolSize-1)\\SequenceLength$ in tokens and dollars.\n(c) The task owner now requires a result within 60 s. Which allocation meets the deadline, and at what bill? A prefix cache would charge re-read prompt tokens at 10 percent of list price. What does breadth then cost, and what omitted term in this accounting should an engineer measure before committing to it?\n\n**2. Sizing a pool against a target (Basic).** For a fixed repair task, independent candidates pass the acceptance check with probability $\\AttemptPassProbability = 0.20$, and the selector returns a passing member whenever one exists.\n\n(a) Find the smallest pool $\\CandidatePoolSize$ whose pass@$\\CandidatePoolSize$ reaches 90 percent, and the availability gained by its last candidate.\n(b) Compare candidates spent per accepted repair for the parallel pool and for sequential retry that stops at the first pass and gives up after $\\CandidatePoolSize$ attempts. What does the parallel pool buy for its premium?\n(c) The workload is actually mixed: half the tasks pass at $0.40$ per candidate and half never pass, so the average rate is still $0.20$. What pool success does the pool size $\\CandidatePoolSize$ from part (a) deliver, and what is the largest pool success any $\\CandidatePoolSize$ can deliver? What should the runtime do with the second half of the workload instead?\n(d) In a benchmark, 4 of 20 samples for one task pass. Compute the subset estimate of pass@5 and compare it with the plug-in value $1-(1-\\PassingSampleCount/\\GeneratedSampleCount)^5$.\n\n**3. How strict must the check be? (Basic).** A candidate pool has valid-candidate rate $\\ValidCandidateRate = 0.10$. The current check has $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate = 0.05$ and $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate = 0.01$ and costs \\$0.10 per run.\n\n(a) Compute the probability of a pass $\\VerifierPassProbability$ and the precision $\\VerifierPrecision$ from @eq-vol3-verifier-precision.\n(b) Precision must reach 95 percent. Find the largest $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate$ that meets the target at this $\\ValidCandidateRate$, and alternatively the smallest $\\ValidCandidateRate$ that meets it with the current check.\n(c) The policy pre-generates 32 candidates and checks them in a fixed order until the first pass, abstaining if none passes. Compute the probability of any selection, the probability that the batch contains at least one false pass, and the probability of selecting a defective candidate. Explain why the second and third differ.\n(d) A stricter check has $\\VerifierFalseAcceptanceRate = 0.005$ and $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate = 0.08$ and costs \\$0.40 per run. A defective selection costs \\$2,000 in incident loss. Compare the two checks on expected incident loss plus expected checking cost per attempt, and decide which to deploy.\n\n**4. What step scoring costs (Intermediate).** A learned process scorer reads a $3{,}000$-token task input and the growing prefix of a candidate built in $\\ScoredStepCount = 20$ steps of 150 tokens each. The runtime scores 64 candidate trajectories per task at the scorer price in @tbl-vol3-test-time-compute-exercise-givens.\n\n(a) Using @eq-vol3-prm-rescoring, compute scorer input per trajectory and per task when every prefix is rescored from scratch, and when the interface supports incremental prefix reuse.\n(b) For this $\\SequenceLength$ and $\\FiniteChange \\ScoredPrefixLength$, beyond how many steps does the quadratic term exceed the $\\ScoredStepCount \\SequenceLength$ term?\n(c) Without prefix reuse, a team proposes scoring only every fourth step. Compute its scorer input and bill per task. A defective step is then caught on average 1.5 steps later, and every pruned trajectory generates those extra steps at the output price. Bound the added generation cost per task, and recommend a scoring stride with and without prefix reuse.\n\n**5. Keeping a tree finite (Intermediate).** Each retained node proposes $\\SearchBranchingFactor = 4$ children of 200 generated tokens, a step check rejects each child independently with probability $\\ChildPruningProbability = 0.60$, and the search runs $\\SearchDepth = 5$ steps deep under a ceiling of $20{,}000$ generated tokens.\n\n(a) Compute $\\EffectiveBranchingFactor$ from @eq-vol3-effective-branching and the expected generated nodes and tokens with and without pruning. Which expected counts fall below the ceiling, and why does an expected count below it not guarantee that every run fits?\n(b) What rejection rate would make the tree die out on its own, and why is a rejection rate that high rarely available?\n(c) The step check also rejects valid steps with probability $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate$. A correct solution needs five consecutive valid steps to survive. Compute its survival probability at $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate = 0.10$, and the largest $\\VerifierFalseRejectionRate$ that keeps survival at or above 80 percent. What does this imply for tuning the check&#x27;s threshold?\n\n**6. A waiting customer sets the policy (Intermediate).** The support agent answers a policy question while the customer waits. Each request carries a $6{,}000$-token prompt of policy excerpts and conversation, each candidate reply is 150 output tokens, and a grounding check against the cited policy takes 1.5 s. A first reply passes with probability $0.70$; conditional on a failed first reply, a revision that reads the check&#x27;s report passes with probability $0.50$. Concurrent candidates are checked concurrently, and the reply must arrive within 8 s.\n\n(a) Compute the duration of one generate-and-check round. How many feedback rounds fit the deadline, and what pass probability does feedback therefore deliver?\n(b) For concurrent breadth of $\\CandidatePoolSize = 1$ to $4$, compute pass probability, model-token bill per request, and bill per passing reply. Find the smallest $\\CandidatePoolSize$ that reaches 95 percent.\n(c) The service can hold 600 concurrent decode streams for all sessions. How many simultaneous sessions does the choice in (b) support, compared with a single candidate? Which resource binds this policy?\n(d) Contrast with the repair scenario (notebook \\ref{nbk-03-strategy-comparison}), where a second feedback round fit the deadline. Name the constraint that changed the answer, and state one design change that would make feedback viable for the support agent.\n\n**7. Scoping a revision (Intermediate).** A repair candidate records generated artifacts. Each is listed here with its output tokens and direct prerequisites: diagnosis $\\text{Dx}$ (300; source excerpt, failure observation), pool patch $\\RepairPatchArtifact_1$ (600; source excerpt, $\\text{Dx}$), metrics patch $\\RepairPatchArtifact_2$ (200; $\\text{Dx}$), check selection $\\text{Chk}$ (150; $\\RepairPatchArtifact_1$, test inventory), API notes $\\text{API}$ (250; API declaration), release note $\\text{Rel}$ (200; $\\RepairPatchArtifact_1$, $\\RepairPatchArtifact_2$, $\\text{API}$), and migration script $\\text{Mig}$ (400; $\\text{API}$). The labels are local names for this problem only.\n\n(a) A waiter-and-return test contradicts $\\text{Dx}$. List $\\text{Desc}(\\text{Dx})$, and compute the output tokens of scoped regeneration (the diagnosis and its descendants) against full regeneration.\n(b) Instead, the API declaration changes while the diagnosis stands. Which artifacts need review, and what share of the output does scoped regeneration avoid?\n(c) The pool patch in fact calls the pool&#x27;s public constructor, so $\\RepairPatchArtifact_1$ depends on the API declaration, but that edge was never recorded. After the declaration changes, which stale artifacts survive the traversal, and what could the runtime then wrongly reuse? Propose how the runtime should record edges so that this failure cannot occur silently, and state what that costs.\n\n**8. A judge under selection pressure (Advanced).** The research agent drafts $\\CandidatePoolSize$ candidate summaries of what it has read, and a learned judge scores each as $\\EstimatedCandidateUtility_i = \\CandidateUtility_i + \\CandidateScoringError_i$. True quality is $\\CandidateUtility_i \\sim \\GaussianDistribution(\\CandidateUtilityMean, \\CandidateUtilityStandardDeviation^2)$ with $\\CandidateUtilityStandardDeviation = 1.0$, scoring error is $\\CandidateScoringError_i \\sim \\GaussianDistribution(0, \\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation^2)$ with $\\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation = 0.5$, all independent. The exact expected maximum of $\\CandidatePoolSize$ standard normal draws is 1.03, 1.77, and 2.34 for $\\CandidatePoolSize$ = 4, 16, and 64. Each candidate costs \\$0.12.\n\n(a) Using the exact values in place of @eq-vol3-argmax-noise, bound the winner&#x27;s expected scoring error at each $\\CandidatePoolSize$.\n(b) Show that when selection is on $\\EstimatedCandidateUtility$, the winner&#x27;s expected quality gain over $\\CandidateUtilityMean$ is $(\\CandidateUtilityStandardDeviation^2/\\EstimatedUtilityStandardDeviation)\\,\\ExpectedNormalMaximum$ and its expected error is $(\\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation^2/\\EstimatedUtilityStandardDeviation)\\,\\ExpectedNormalMaximum$, where $\\EstimatedUtilityStandardDeviation = \\sqrt{\\CandidateUtilityStandardDeviation^2 + \\ScoringErrorStandardDeviation^2}$ and $\\ExpectedNormalMaximum$ is the expected standard-normal maximum. Evaluate both at the three pool sizes, and compare quality gained per dollar in moving from 4 to 16 and from 16 to 64 candidates.\n(c) A fraction $\\ExploitCandidateFraction = 0.002$ of summaries cite a retrieved page whose planted text the judge rewards but which does not support the claim. Compute the probability that a pool of 4, 16, and 64 contains at least one such summary, and explain why selection then tends to return it.\n(d) Redesign the selection policy. Choose a pool size and a check the planted page cannot influence, and state what evidence the returned summary must carry. How does the binding constraint here differ from the repair agent&#x27;s?\n\n**9. Stopping before an irreversible change (Advanced).** The remediation agent generates candidate configuration changes in rounds, each costing \\$0.20 in generation and a staging check. Calibrated on held-out incidents, the estimated probability of a staging-accepted candidate after $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$ rounds is $\\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_{\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount}$ = 0.40, 0.58, 0.68, 0.74, 0.77, 0.79 for $\\AdmittedWorkUnitCount$ = 1 to 6, with $\\CalibratedAcceptanceEstimate_0 = 0$, and the hard ceiling is six rounds.\n\n(a) For each prospective round, derive the conditional acceptance gain given no earlier pass and the increment in expected stopped-policy cost. Compute the marginal ratio of @eq-vol3-marginal-stopping. With $\\MarginalStoppingThreshold = 0.25$ per dollar, how many rounds can the policy admit after earlier failures, and what are its acceptance probability and expected generation-and-check expenditure if it stops at the first accepted candidate?\n(b) An accepted automated change saves \\$30 of on-call time compared with escalation. Derive $\\MarginalStoppingThreshold$ from that value, and state how many rounds the policy now admits and which rule ends the search.\n(c) A colleague proposes that when the ceiling is reached without an accepted candidate, the agent applies its highest-scoring candidate. That candidate is defective with probability 0.5, a defective change costs \\$40,000, and escalating to a human costs \\$150. Compute each fallback&#x27;s expected loss conditional on reaching the ceiling without an accepted candidate, then its contribution per initiated task under the policy in (a). Treat deliberation expenditure as common to both alternatives. Identify the stopping-precedence rule the proposal violates.\n(d) Write the redesigned policy as the ordered checks the runtime applies before admitting another round, including what it returns in each case. Why does a staging-accepted candidate still not authorize the production change?\n\n::: {.callout-chapter-connection title=&quot;From deliberation to working state&quot;}\nDeliberation leaves candidate versions, failed hypotheses, observations, and dependency records. The next model call cannot treat all of them as equally current or useful, even when every one of them still fits in its window. It needs a working context that retains the evidence supporting the selected candidate, distinguishes superseded material, and carries unresolved questions forward. @Sec-vol3-context-engineering develops this logical state-selection problem. Only after deciding what belongs in the working context does the physical question arise of how to store and reuse its model state, developed in @sec-vol3-kv-cache.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>\\part{key:vol3_memory}</p>", "quizzes": []};
